#import "let_bindings.typ": *
#import "void-template/1.0.0/void-import.typ": *

= Sheaf theory

== The affine flag variety and $I$-equivariant sheaves

#def()[
	Let $I subset L^+G$ denote the Iwahori subgroup of $L G$. We define the affine flag variety to be $Fl=L G\/I$. Obviously, there is a projection map $p:L G->L G\/I$. 

	We define $D_I (Fl)$ to be the derived category of $I$-equivariant constructible $ell$-adic sheaves. 
]

#const(label-name: "Const_equi_conv_product")[
	For a group $G$ acting on schemes $G lact Y$ and $X ract G$, and sheaves $cal(F) in Sh(X\/G)$, $cal(G) in Sh(G\\Y)$, we may define the $cal(F)times.square_G cal(G) in Sh(X times^G Y)$ via the following process: consider the morphism 
	$ i:(X\/G)times(G\\Y)->(X\/G)times_(BB G)(G\\Y)=X times^G Y $
	and we define $cal(F)times.square_G cal(G)=i^!(cal(F)times.square cal(G))$.  

	Then, if we have some $G$-equivariant multiplication map $m:X times Y->Z$, we define the convolution product 
	$ cal(F)*_G cal(G)=m_*(cal(F)times.square_G cal(G)) $
	Such convolution product is naturally associative for three spaces able to do convolution. 

	In particular, such formalism will be compatible with the pro-group acting on ind-scheme formalism, since we may do the convolution for finite type groups on finite type schemes by passing to the presentation. 
]

In our case, $X, Y, Z$ will be sets of the form $I w I$ or $I w I\/I$, and the multiplication maps are given obviously. Moreover, $m$ will be ind-proper, so we may define the convolution via $m_!$ rather than $m_*$, which we will use later on. 

#const()[
	Recall we have the extended Weyl group $W^ext=X_*(T)times.r W_f$, with the action of $W_f$ on $X_*(T)$ is by the natural action of the Weyl group on the coweights. We have a length function making $W^ext$ a quasi-coxeter group. Namely, we have it defined by  $ell:W^ext->NN$, given by for $w=x t_lambda$ where $x in W_f$ and $lambda in X_*(T)$
	$ ell(w)=sum_(alpha in R^+\ x(alpha) in R^+)|chevron.l lambda,alpha chevron.r|+sum_(alpha in R^+\ x(alpha) in R^-)|chevron.l lambda, alpha chevron.r+1| $
	This gives $W^ext$ the structure of a quasi-coxeter group. In particular, $W^ext$ is generated by elements of length 1 and 0. 
	
	We let $attach(W, tl: f)$ denote the set of minimal length representatives of the cosets $W\/W_f$. 
	
	Then, we have the decomposition 
	$ L G=product.co_(w in W^ext) I w I=product.co_(w in W^ext)Fl_w $
	and hence the $I$-orbits of $Fl$ are indexed by $W^ext$. In particular, we have $Fl_w =AA^ell(w)$ (this is because the finite parts of $I$ are affine). Moreover, we define $L G_w=p^(-1)(Fl_w)=I w I$ where $p:L G->Fl$ denotes the projection. 
]

#const()[
	We let $j_w:Fl_w->Fl$ denote the inclusion of strata. We consider the following sheaves in $Perv_I (Fl)$ (which we will denote by $cal(P)_I$ for short) 
	$ j_(w!)=j_(w!)(overline(QQ)_ell [ell(w)]),j_(w*)=j_(w*)(overline(QQ)_ell [ell(w)]),L_w=j_(w!*)(overline(QQ)_ell [ell(w)]) $
	where $overline(QQ)_ell [ell(w)]$ is the shifted constant sheaf as a perverse sheaf on $Fl_w$. We define
	$ attach(cal(P)_I, tl: f)=cal(P)_I\/Span{L_w|w in.not attach(W, tl:f)} $ 
	We denote the projection $cal(P)_I->attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f)$ by $cal(F) mapsto attach(cal(F),tl:f)$

	Likewise, in the mixed setting, we have the sheaves in $Perv_(I,mixed)(Fl)$
	$ j_(w!)^m=j_(w!)(overline(QQ)_ell [ell(w)](ell(w)/2)),j_(w*)^m=j_(w*)(overline(QQ)_ell [ell(w)](ell(w)/2)) $
	We have the simple objects (as below) pure in this setting, because it is an intermediate extension of a pure sheaf. 
	$ L_w^m=j_(w!*)(overline(QQ)_ell [ell(w)](ell(w)/2)) $
] 

Note that in particular we have $j_(e!)=j_(e*)=delta_e$, the skyscraper at the unit coset for $e in W$ is the unit element. 

We begin with some basic properties of these sheaves. 

#lem(label-name: "Lem_conv_I_equi_shv")[
	+	For $w_1, w_2 in W^ext$ with $ell(w_1 w_2)=ell(w_1)+ell(w_2)$, we have #h(1fr)
		$ j_(w_1 !)*_I j_(w_2 !)tilde.eq j_(w_1 w_2 !) $
		and for $w_1, w_2, w_3 in W^ext$ such that $ell(w_1 w_2 w_3)=ell(w_1)+ell(w_2)+ell(w_3)$, the above isomorphism is compatible with the associativity of the convolution. 
	+	The likewise result holds for $j_(w *)$. 
	+	For any $w in W^ext$, there are canonical isomorphisms 
		$ j_(w!)*_I j_(w^(-1)*)tilde.eq delta_e tilde.eq j_(w^(-1)*)*_I j_(w !) $
] 
#proof[
	+	By the formalism of Tits systems, for $ell(w_1 w_2)=ell(w_1)+ell(w_2)$, we have the equation of subsets $I w_1 I dot.c I w_2 I=I w_1 w_2 I$, and in particular, we have 
		$ L G_(w_1)times^I Fl_(w_2)=Fl_(w_1 w_2) $
		then we have 
		$ j_(w_1 w_2 !)=j_(w_1 w_2 !)(overline(QQ)_ell)[ell(w_1 w_2)]=j_(w_1 w_2 !)m_! (overline(QQ)_ell [ell(w_1)] times.square_I overline(QQ)_ell [ell(w_2)])=j_(w_1 !)*_I j_(w_2 !) $
	+	Similar to the previous proof. 
	+	It suffices to prove the assertion for $w$ a simple reflection and for $w$ of length 0, by the associativity of the convolution product and the previous statements. To do this, we verify the stalks of the sheaf $j_(w!)*_I j_(w^(-1)*)$ vanishes outside $I$. 
	
		We compute the stalks of $j_(x!)*_I j_(y*)$. Since it is by definition $I$-equivariant, it suffices to compute its stalks at $z I$ where $z in W^ext$. We temporarily forget the equivariance, and regard the convolution as a sheaf on $Fl$ (rather than $I\\Fl$). By base change theorem, we have 
		$ (j_(x!)*_I j_(y*))_(z I)=m_!(j_(x!) times.square_I j_(y*))_(z I)=R Gamma_c (m^(-1)(z I),(j_(x!)times.square_I j_(y*))|_(m^(-1)(z I))) $
		We have an isomorphism $Fl->m^(-1)(z I)$ given by $g I mapsto (g,g^(-1)z I)$. We consider the following diagram 
		// https://q.uiver.app/#r=typst&
		#align(center, diagram({
			node((-1, 1), [$X$])
			node((1, 1), [$Fl$])
			node((1, -1), [$L G$])
			node((-1, -1), [$z I y^(-1) I$])
			node((0, -2), [$L G times I y I$])
			node((2, -2), [$L G times Fl$])
			node((2, 0), [$L G times ^I Fl$])
			node((0, 0), [$L G times^I I y I$])
			edge((-1, 1), (0, 0), "->")
			edge((-1, 1), (1, 1), [$j_(z,y)$], label-side: right, "->")
			edge((1, 1), (2, 0), [$f$], label-side: right, "->")
			edge((0, 0), (2, 0), "->")
			edge((-1, -1), (-1, 1), "->")
			edge((1, -1), (1, 1), "->")
			edge((0, -2), (0, 0), "->")
			edge((2, -2), (2, 0), "->")
			edge((1, -1), (2, -2), [$tilde(f)$], label-side: left, "->")
			edge((-1, -1), (0, -2), "->")
			edge((0, -2), (2, -2), "->")
			edge((-1, -1), (1, -1), "->")
		}))
		where the map $tilde(f)$ is given by $g mapsto (g,g^(-1)z I)$. Thus we may compute $(j_(x!)times.square_I j_(y*))|_(m^(-1)(z I))=f^*(j_(x!)times.square_I j_(y*))$ as follows. We first forget the $I$-equivariant structure on all sheaves, which allows us to pass to the upper level of the above diagram. Then, we have 
		$
			tilde(f)^*(j_(x!)times.square j_(y*)) =j_(x!)times.o f_2^*j_(y*)=j_(x!)times.o tilde(j)_(z,y*)overline(QQ)_ell [ell(y)]
		$
		which is because $tilde(j)_(z,y):z I y^(-1) I inj L G$ is precisely the pullback of $tilde(j)_y:I y I inj L G$ along the lift of $tilde(f)_2$, namely $g mapsto g^(-1)z$. Note that this is an isomorphism, so $*$-pushforward commutes with the corresponding $*$-pullbacks. Thus, we have the formula
		$ f^*(j_(x!)times.square_I j_(y*))=j_(x!)times.o j_(z,y*)overline(QQ)_ell [ell(y)] $

		Then, by the projection formula, we have 
		$ j_(x!)times.o j_(z,y*)overline(QQ)_ell [ell(y)]=j_(x!)(overline(QQ)_ell [ell(x)]times.o j_x^*j_(z,y*)overline(QQ)_ell [ell(y)])=j_(x!)j_x^*j_(z,y*)overline(QQ)_ell [ell(x)+ell(y)] $
		and hence a corresponding formula for compactly supported cohomology 
		$ R Gamma_c (m^(-1)(z I),(j_(x!)times.square_I j_(y*))|_(m^(-1)(z I)))=R Gamma_c (Fl_x,j_x^* j_(z,y*)overline(QQ)_ell)[ell(x)+ell(y)] $
		Now, substituting $x=w$, $y=w^(-1)$, and $z=e$, we have $X=Fl_w$ and hence
		$ (j_(w!)*_I j_(w^(-1)*))_I=R Gamma_c (Fl_w,overline(QQ)_ell)[2ell(w)]=R Gamma_c (AA^(ell(w)),overline(QQ)_ell)[2ell(w)]=overline(QQ)_ell $
		Then, for $w=x=y in S$, note that $j_(w!)*_I j_(w*)$ has support in $Fl_s product.co Fl_e=overline(Fl)_s=PP^1$, by the property of proper pushforwards. Thus, it suffices to prove that $j_(w!)*_I j_(w*)$ has no support on $w I$. Note $j_(w):X inj Fl_w$ can be identified as $GG_m inj AA^1$, removing the $0$ (or $oo$, if we first embed $Fl_w inj PP^1$ avoiding the 0). Hence, it suffices to prove that $R Gamma_c (AA^1,j_*(overline(QQ)_ell)_(AA^1-0))=0$, which is well-known. 

		Finally, for $ell(w)=0$, $j_(w!)=j_(w*)=delta_w$ is again the skyscraper at $w I$, so obviously the assertion holds. Thus we have proven $(3)$. 
]

#coro(label-name: "Coro_conv_I_equi_shv_mixed")[
	The above lemma holds with $j_(w!)$ replaced by $j_(w!)^m$, and $j_(w*)$ replaced by $j_(w*)^m$. 
]
#proof[
	By doing the same process on the finite level $(Fl_w)_0$ gives the result. 
]

#lem(label-name: "Lem_num_id_I_equi")[
	+	For $w in W^aff$, we have non-trivial morphisms #h(1fr)
		$ delta_e->j_(w!),j_(w*)->delta_e $
		whose (co)kernel does not contain $delta_e$ in its Jordan-Hölder series. 
	+	If $w = w_1 w_2 in W^ext$, $w_2 in W^aff$, and $ell(w)=ell(w_1)+ell(w_2)$, then
		$ dim Hom(j_(w_1 !),j_(w!))=1=dim Hom(j_(w*),j_(w_1 *)) $
		and the unique non-zero map is injective (surjective). 
] 
#proof[
	We only prove the statements for $j_w!$, and the other side is obtained by Verdier duality. 
	+	For $w in W^aff$ of length 1 (i.e. a simple reflection), note $overline(Fl)_w=PP^1$, so we have an exact sequence
		$ 0->delta_e->j_(w!)->L_w->0 $
		Then for general $u in W^aff$, we proceed by induction on $ell(u)$. Suppose $w$ is a simple reflection such that $ell(u w)$, taking convolution $j_(u!)*_I (-)$ gives a triangle  
		$ j_(u!)->j_(u w!)->j_(u!)*_I L_w $
		We first prove the final term is a perverse sheaf. We let $I_w=I union.sq I w I$ denote the parahoric subgroup of $L G$. Then, since $overline(Fl)_w=PP^1$, we have $pi:L G\/I->>L G\/I_w$ a $PP^1$-fibration. Notice that on $PP^1$, we have $j_(!*)(overline(QQ)_ell [1])=overline(QQ)_ell [1]$, where $j:AA^1->PP^1$ is the inclusion. Thus, we may compute $j_(u!)*_I L_w=pi^*pi_*(j_(u!))[1]$. Then, since $pi compose j_u$ is a locally closed affine embedding, we have $pi^*pi_*(j_(u!))[1]$ a perverse sheaf. Hence, the above triangle is an exact sequence of perverse sheaves. Now, since $j_(u!)$ only contains one $delta_e$, it suffices to show that $j_(u!)*_I L_w$ contains no $delta_e$. This is because the subquotients of $pi^*pi_*(j_(u!))[1]$ is of the form $pi^*(L[1])$ (by adjunction, since $pi$ is proper, we have $pi^*$ both left and right adjoint to $pi_*$), hence contains no $delta_e$.
	+	we have
		$ Hom(j_(w_1 !),j_(w!))=Hom(j_(w_1 !),j_(w_1 !)*_I j_(w_2 !))=Hom(delta_e, j_(w_2 !)) $ 
		since $j_(w_1^(-1)*)*_I j_(w_1 !)=delta_e$. So (2) follows from (1). 
]

With these preparations, we may define the most important sheaves in $Perv_I (Fl)$ we are considering in this paper. 

#const()[
	The functor $lambda mapsto j_(lambda *)$ for $lambda in X_*^+(T)$ actually extends to $lambda in X_*(T)$. Indeed, for $lambda in X_*(T)$ (or $X^*(T^or)$), we let $lambda=mu-rho$ for $mu,nu in X_*^+(T)$. Then, we define $J_lambda=j_(t_mu*)*_I j_(t_(-nu) !)$. These objects are called Wakimoto sheaves. By definition, we have $J_(lambda_1+lambda_2)=J_(lambda_1)*_I J_(lambda_2)$ by the previous lemma on the computations of convolutions of $j$'s. 
]

#lem(label-name: "Lem_Wakimoto_hom_partial_ord")[
	We have $RHom(J_lambda,J_mu)=0$ unless $lambda<=mu$, and when $lambda=mu$, $RHom(J_mu,J_mu)=overline(QQ)_ell$. 
] 
#proof[
	By @Lem_conv_I_equi_shv, convolution against $J_nu$ is invertible. So, we pick $nu>>0$ such that $nu+lambda$ and $nu+mu$ are dominant. Hence we have 
	$ RHom(J_lambda,J_mu)=RHom(J_(nu+lambda),J_(nu+mu))=RHom(j_(nu+lambda,*),j_(nu+mu,*)) $
	and the computation of the final Ext groups are well-known. 
]

With some further work, we may compute the cohomology of Wakimoto sheaves through a similar process. 

#prop(label-name: "Prop_Waki_cohom", [Lemma 4.5.1@CentralShvAR])[
	For any $lambda$ we have $H^n (Fl,J_lambda)=overline(QQ)_ell$ if $n=chevron.l lambda,2rho chevron.r$ and 0 otherwise. In particular, for $lambda$ a coroot, we have the cohomology of $J_lambda$ concentrated in even degrees. 
]

#const()[
	Moreover, we may define the Wakimoto sheaves not just for $lambda in X_*(T)$, but for general $w in W^ext$. Let $w = t_lambda dot.c w_f$, we may define $J_w = J_lambda *_I j_(w_f *)$ (sorry about changing right $T_*(X)$ cosets to left cosets here, but we will not use the generalized Wakimoto sheaves too much). 
]

#lem(label-name: "Lem_Wakimoto_perverse")[
	The Wakimoto sheaves $J_w$ for $w in W^ext$ are perverse. 
] 
#proof[
	We first take $w=t_lambda^(-1)t_mu w_f$, where $lambda,mu in X_*^+(T)$, and $w_f in W_f$. Thus, we have 
	$ J_w=j_(t_(-lambda) !)*_I j_(t_mu w_f *) $
	Then, unwrapping the definition of the convolution map, we have 
	$ J_w=m_!(j_(t_(-lambda) !)times.square_I j_(t_mu w_f,*))=m_! (j_(t_(-lambda))times^I id_Fl)_! (overline(QQ)_ell [w(-lambda)]times.square_I j_(t_mu w_f,*)) $
	But notice that the composition of first two morphism is exactly the restricted multiplication map $m:Fl_(-lambda) times^I Fl->Fl$, which is an affine morphism. But notice that $f_!$ for an affine morphism is perverse left $t$-exact, so $J_w in attach(D^(>=0)(Fl),tl:p)$. Dually, by writing 
	$ J_w=m_*(id_(Fl)times^I j_(t_mu w_f))_*(j_(t_(-lambda),!)times.square_I overline(QQ)_ell [ell(t_mu w_f)]) $
	we have $f_*$ for an affine morphism perverse right $t$-exact, hence combining with the above fact we have $J_w$ a perverse sheaf. 
]

For the general Wakimoto sheaves $J_w$, we have the following lemma. 

#lem(label-name: "Lem_Waki_sym_mon_RepT")[
	We have $J_lambda *_I J_w=J_(t_lambda w)$ for $lambda in X_*(T)$ and $w in W_f$. 
]
#proof[
	This follows from the definition of the generalized Wakimoto sheaves. 
]

== Gaitsgory's central functor 

We now give a review of Gaitsgory's paper @CentralFct. In this section, we set $k$ to be either $FF_q$ or $overline(FF)_q$, and all schemes considered will be over this $k$. We will construct a central functor 
$ Z:Perv_(L^+G)(Gr)->Perv_I (Fl) $ 
along with the following properties: 

#thm([Theorem 1@CentralFct], label-name: "Thm_central1")[
	There exists a functor 
	$ Z:Perv_(L^+G)(Gr)->Perv_I (Fl) $
	with the following properties: 
	+	For any $cal(F) in Perv_(L^+G)(Gr)$, $Z(cal(F))$ is convolution exact (see @Def_conv_exact_shf). 
	+	The image of $Z$ is central, that is, there is a natural isomorphism of functors 
		$ Z(-)*_I (-)tilde.eq (-)*_I Z(-):Perv_(L^+G)(Gr)times.o Perv_I (Fl)->Perv_I (Fl) $
	+	$Z$ is a monoidal functor between the convolution monoidal structures. 
	+	The projection $pi_!:Perv_I (Fl)->Perv_(L^+G)(Gr)$ is a right inverse for $Z$. 
]

Notice that the properties of $Z$ for $k=overline(FF)_q$ gives the properties for $ell$-adic sheaves, and $k=FF_q$ gives the properties for mixed sheaves. 

#thm([Theorem 2@CentralFct], label-name:"Thm_central2")[
	The functor $Z$ is equipped with a nilpotent monodromy endomorphism 
	$ M:Z(-)->Z(-)(-1) #[or] M:Z(-)->Z(-) $
	(the first endomorphism is for $k=FF_q$, where $(-1)$ denotes the Tate twist in the mixed case, and the second endomorphism is for $k=overline(FF)_q$) which is a tensor derivation of $Z$. That is, the following diagram commutes 
	// https://q.uiver.app/#r=typst&
	#align(center, diagram({
		node((-1, 0), [$Z(cal(F)_1)*_I Z(cal(F)_2)$])
		node((-1, 1), [$Z(cal(F)_1*_(L^+G)cal(F)_2)$])
		node((1, 0), [$Z(cal(F)_1)*_I Z(cal(F)_2)(-1)$])
		node((1, 1), [$Z(cal(F)_1*_(L^+G)cal(F)_2)(-1)$])
		edge((-1, 0), (-1, 1), [$tilde.eq$], label-side: left, "->")
		edge((1, 0), (1, 1), [$tilde.eq$], label-side: left, "->")
		edge((-1, 0), (1, 0), [$M_(cal(F)_1)*id+id*M_(cal(F)_2)$], label-side: left, "->")
		edge((-1, 1), (1, 1), [$M_(cal(F)_1*cal(F_2))$], label-side: left, "->")
	}))
	The likewise square commutes for $k=overline(FF)_q$, with the Tate twists removed. 
]

These functors will be crucial to constructing the equivalence. We will construct this functor via nearby cycles, and prove centrality, which is analogous to the braiding of the category $Perv_(L^+G)(Gr)$, via fusion. For these purposes, we fix a smooth curve $X\/k$ (not necessarily proper) in this subsection. We first recall the definition of the Beilinson-Drinfeld Grassmannian. 

#def()[
	We define $Gr_X$ to be the functor whose $S$-points are given by the set of triples $(y,cal(F)_G,beta)$, where $y in X$ is an $S$-point, $cal(F)_G$ a $G$-bundle over $X times S$, and $beta$ is a trivialization of $cal(F)_G$ outside the graph of $y:S->X$. 
]

We slightly modify the above definition to obtain a factorizable version of the flag variety. 

#def()[
	We fix a point $x in X$. We define $Fl_X$ to be the ind-scheme whose $S$-points are given by quadruples $(y,cal(F)_G,beta,epsilon)$, where $(y,cal(F),beta)$ are as above, and $epsilon$ is a reduction of $cal(F)_G|_(x times S)$ to a $B$-bundle. 
]

#prop(label-name: "Prop_const_deformation_Fl_Gr")[
	We have a projection $Fl_X->>Gr_X$. Moreover, $Fl_(X-x)tilde.eq Gr_(X-x) times G\/B$, and $Fl_x=Fl$. 
] 
#proof[
	This is straightforward from the above moduli description. 
]

#const(label-name: "Const_nearby_cyc")[
	We first construct the nearby cycle functor used in this section. We fix $x in X$ a $k$-point. We let $cal(O)$ denote the Henselization of the local ring $cal(O)_(X,x)$. Then, let the geometric generic point and the geometric special point of $cal(O)$ be given by $overline(eta)$ and $overline(s)$ respectively. We let $overline(cal(O))$ denote the integral closure of $cal(O)$ in the algebraic closure of its fraction field $overline(K)$. 

	Then, for a scheme $Y->X$, consider the following pullback diagram // https://q.uiver.app/#r=typst&
	#align(center, diagram({
		node((0, 1), [$Spec overline(cal(O))$])
		node((-1, 1), [$overline(s)$])
		node((1, 1), [$overline(eta)$])
		node((0, 0), [$Y_(overline(cal(O)))$])
		node((1, 0), [$Y_(overline(eta))$])
		node((-1, 0), [$Y_(overline(s))$])
		edge((0, 0), (0, 1), "->")
		edge((-1, 1), (0, 1), [$i$], label-side: left, "->")
		edge((1, 1), (0, 1), [$j$], label-side: right, "->")
		edge((-1, 0), (0, 0), [$overline(dotless.i)$], label-side: left, "->")
		edge((1, 0), (0, 0), [$overline(dotless.j)$], label-side: right, "->")
		edge((1, 0), (1, 1), "->")
		edge((-1, 0), (-1, 1), "->")
	}))
	We now construct the nearby cycles functor $Psi:D(Y_(X-x))->D(Y_x)$ for $k=FF_q$. For a sheaf $cal(F) in D(Y_(X-x))$, we define 
	$ Psi'(cal(F))=overline(dotless.i)^*overline(dotless.j)_*(cal(F)|_(Y_(overline(eta))))[-1]in D(Y_(overline(s))) $
	Note $Y_(overline(s))->Y_s$ is a Galois covering with Galois group $Gal(overline(k)\/k)$, so we need a final action of $Gal(overline(k)\/k)$ on $Psi'(cal(F))$ to descend it to a sheaf in $D(Y_s)=D(Y_x)$. For this, notice that the Galois group $Gal(overline(K)\/K)$ acts on $overline(eta)$, so acts on $cal(F)|_(Y_(overline(eta)))$, and hence on $Psi'(cal(F))$. Also, we have an exact sequence 
	$ 1->I->Gal(overline(K)\/K)->Gal(overline(k)\/k)->1 $
	we thus fix a splitting of this exact sequence $iota:Gal(overline(k)\/k)->Gal(overline(K)\/K)$, and thus define 
	$ Psi(cal(F))=(Psi'(cal(F)),iota(Gal(overline(k)\/k)))in D(Y_x) $
	where the pair is the descent datum of the Galois action. 

	Moreover, there is an extra inertia action $I lact Psi(cal(F))$. We define the unipotent nearby cycles $Psi^unip (cal(F))$ to be the subsheaf of $Psi(cal(F))$ where $I$ acts unipotently (recall a priori a finite index subgroup of $I$ acts unipotently on $Psi(cal(F))$ by the Grothendieck's monodromy theorem). We only consider the pro-$ell$ part of the tame inertia, since we are working with $ell$-adic sheaves. So we have a unipotent action $ZZ_ell (1) lact Psi^unip (cal(F))$. Then, we may take its $ell$-adic logarithm and obtain an $ZZ_ell$-module morphism 
	$ ZZ_ell (1)->End(Psi^unip (cal(F))) $
	By tensor-Hom adjunction, we have thus constructed a monodromy endomorphism 
	$ M:Psi^unip (cal(F))->Psi^unip (cal(F))(-1) $

	The most important aspect of this functor is that it preserves perverse sheaves (see Section 4.4@PerverseBBDG). That is, we have $Psi^unip$ restricts to a functor 
	$ Psi^unip: Perv(Y_(X-x))->Perv(Y_x) $
	Since we will not use the non-unipotent version later, we will not use $Psi$, and so we replace $Psi^unip$ by $Psi$. Moreover, in the case we are concerned with, we will have $Psi=Psi^unip$. 

	Notice that in particular, the nearby cycles construction preserves the category of mixed sheaves by the same paper@PerverseBBDG. Thus, as a result, the later constructed functor $Z$ also preserves mixed sheaves. 

	In the case of $k=overline(FF)_q$, the $ZZ_ell$ still exists, but since the Galois group $Gal(overline(k)\/k)$ is trivial, there is no data of the Tate twist. In this case, we obtain a plain nilpotent endomorphism $Z(-)->Z(-)$. 
] 

#const()[
	We then consider some factorization constructions. We let $Aut$ denote the Virasoro group, which is the ind-group scheme of automorphisms of $k[[t]]$. Thus by definition, $Aut$ acts on $L^+ G$, $L G$, and hence on $Gr$. 

	By definition, we have 
	$ Aut(k)={a_0+a_1 t+dots.c in k[[t]] | #[$a_0$nilpotent, $a_1$ invertible]} $
	with multiplication given by composition. We define $Aut_0$ to be its subgroup of series with $a_0=0$. 
	
	For any curve $X$, we may construct an $Aut_0$-torsor over $X$ called the coordinate scheme. The scheme $Coord_X$ has points given by the moduli of $(x,t_x)$, where $x in X$, and $t_x in cal(O)^and_(X,x)$ is a topological generator. This space moreover admits a $Aut$-action by noticing $Coord_X|_(DD_x)=Aut$ as a torsor, so gluing this construction together gives an $Aut$-action on $Coord_X$. 

	By definition, we have $Gr_X=Coord_X times^Aut Gr$. 
]

#const()[
	Finally we may construct the central functor. We first consider the composition of functors 
	$ Z:Perv_Aut (Gr)-->^(cal(O)*_Aut (-))Perv (Gr_(X-x))-->^((-)times.square delta_(e_(G\/B)))Perv (Fl_(X-x))-->^(Psi_(X))Perv (Fl) $
	We then show it upgrades to a functor $Perv_(L^+ G)(Gr)->Perv_I (Fl)$. First, notice any $L^+ G$-equivariant sheaf in $Gr$ is naturally $Aut$-equivariant since $Perv_(L^+ G)(Gr)$ is semisimple, and any simple sheaf is by definition $Aut$-equivariant. Then, we take an equivariant perverse sheaf $cal(F) in Perv_(L^+ G)(Gr)$. We consider $Supp Z(cal(F))$, and note $I$ must act through a quotient $I_k$ (here $I_k$ denotes the preimage of $B$ in $L^k G$ rather than $L^+ G$) on $Supp Z(cal(F))$, so it suffices to prove that $Z(cal(F))$ is $I_k$-equivariant. 

	To prove this, we first recall that $I$ is the pullback group 
	// https://q.uiver.app/#r=typst&q=WzAsNCxbMSwwLCJCIl0sWzEsMSwiRyJdLFswLDEsIkxeK0ciXSxbMCwwLCJJIl0sWzIsMV0sWzAsMV0sWzMsMF0sWzMsMl1d
	#align(center, diagram({
		node((1, 0), [$B$])
		node((1, 1), [$G$])
		node((0, 1), [$L^+G$])
		node((0, 0), [$I$])
		edge((0, 1), (1, 1), "->")
		edge((1, 0), (1, 1), "->")
		edge((0, 0), (1, 0), "->")
		edge((0, 0), (0, 1), "->")
	}))
	Since we may identify $L^+G=Hom(k[[t]],G)$ with the Hom stack, we may define $I^glob$ to be the group functor sending a scheme $S$ to the maps from the localization of $X times S$ around $x times S$ to $G$, such that the restriction to $x times S$ is mapped to $B$. This can be regarded as the "global" version of $I$, and admits a map $I^glob->I$ given by taking the formal power expansion at $x times S$. Clearly, $I^glob->I->>I_k$ is a surjection. Thus, it suffices to prove $I^glob$-equivariance. However, $I^glob$ admits the above global definition, so we take an open set $U subset X times S$ containing $x times S$, and consider a map $U->G$ restricting to a map $x times S->B$. By the compatibility of nearby cycles with smooth pullbacks, it suffices to prove for the following maps $phi_1,phi_2:U times_X Fl_X->Fl_X$, 
	$ Psi_(U times_X Fl_X)(phi_1^*(cal(F)_(X-x)times.square delta_e_(G\/B)))tilde.eq Psi_(U times_X Fl_X)(phi_2^*(cal(F)_(X-x)times.square delta_e_(G\/B))) $
	where $phi_1$ is the projection, and $phi_2$, informally, is the action of $I^glob$ through $U$ on $Fl_X$. The verification of the above equivalence is done by the $L^+G$-equivariance. Readers interested in the details can refer to Proposition 4 of the original paper @CentralFct. 
]

#def()[
	We now construct the global action flag variety that allows us to prove convolution exactness and centrality. We define $Fl_X^act$ to be the functor sending $S$ to quadruples $(y,cal(F)_G,beta',epsilon)$, where $(y,cal(F)_G,epsilon)$ is as in the definition in $Fl_X$, and $beta'$ now is a trivialization of $cal(F)_G$ outside $Gamma_y union (x times S)$. 
]

#prop[
	We have $Fl^act_(X-x)tilde.eq Gr_(X-x) times Fl$, and $Fl^act_x tilde.eq Fl$. 
]
#proof[
	Likewise to the proof of the previous proposition. 
]

#const[
	We now construct the central isomorphism. We define a functor 
	$ cal(C):Perv_Aut (Gr)times Perv(Fl)->Perv(Fl) $
	given by the composition 
	$ Perv_Aut (Gr) times Perv(Fl)-->^((-)_(X-x)times.square(-))Perv(Fl_(X-x)^act)-->^(Psi_(Fl^act))Perv(Fl) $
	Then, Gaitsgory constructed isomorphisms 
	$ Z(cal(F))*_I cal(G) tilde.eq cal(C)(cal(F),cal(G))tilde.eq cal(G)*_I Z(cal(F)) $
	which gives the first two desired properties (see the proof of Proposition 6 @CentralFct). 
]

#def[
	We finally construct the global convolution flag variety that allows us to prove the monoidality of the central functor. Recall we have 
	$ Fl^conv=Fl times^I I\\Fl $ 
	is used in the definition of the convolution product of two sheaves. So, we make the following definition. We let $Fl_X^conv$ denote the functor sending $S$ to the septuples $(y,cal(F)_G,tilde(beta),epsilon,cal(F)_G^1,beta^1,epsilon^1)$, where $y:S->X$ is an $S$-point, $cal(F)_G$ and $cal(F)_G^1$ are $G$-torsors, $beta^1$ a trivialization of $cal(F)_G^1$ outside $Gamma_y$, $tilde(beta)$ an isomorphism of $cal(F)_G$ and $cal(F)_G^1$ outside $Gamma_y$, and finally $epsilon$ and $epsilon^1$ are reductions of $cal(F)_G$ and $cal(F)_G^1$ to $B$ at $x times S$. 
]

This time, we have identifications 
$ Fl_(X-x)^conv=Gr^conv_(X-x)times G\/B times G\/ B,Fl_x^conv=Fl^conv $

#const[
	For the monoidality of $Z$, it suffices to prove 
	$ Psi_(Fl_X^conv)(cal(F)^1_(X-x)times.square cal(F)^2_(X-x)times.square delta_(e_(G\/B))times.square delta_(e_(G\/B)))tilde.eq Z(cal(F)^1)tilde(times.square)Z(cal(F)^2)in Perv(Fl^conv) $
	which is likewise to the above constructions. In particular, we will use that taking nearby cycles commutes with taking proper pushforwards. 
]

For (4) of @Thm_central1, this simply follows from the compatibility of nearby cycles with proper pushforwards, namely by applying this fact to the projection $pi:Fl_X->Gr_X$. In particular, since after pushing forward we are doing nearby cycles with respect to a trivial family, the functor clearly gives $id$. This functor preserves weights, so $pi_!(Z(-))=id$ holds even in the mixed setting. 

We have finished the review of the proof of Theorem 1 in the paper @CentralFct. The fact that $Z$ admits a tensor derivation $M$ follows from the fact that the compatibility of nearby cycles with proper pushforwards respects the action of the inertia group. 

== The Wakimoto filtration 

Now, we construct a filtration on the above central functor by Wakimoto sheaves. More explicitly, we have the following proposition. 

#prop(label-name: "Prop_fil_cent_Wakimoto")[
	We identify the categories $Perv_(L^+G)(Gr)tilde.eq Rep G^or$. Also, we fix a total ordering $<=^t$ on $X_*(T)$ compatible with the group structure and with the partial ordering $<=$. Then, for any $V in Rep G^or$, the sheaf $Z(V)$ has a unique filtration indexed by $(X_*(T),<=^t)$, such that the associated graded pieces satisfies 
	$ gr_nu (Z(V)):=Z(V)_(<=^t nu)\/Z(V)_(<^t nu)=J_nu times.o W_V^nu $
	where $J_nu$ is the Wakimoto sheaves, and $W_V^nu$ is some finite dimensional vector space. 
] 

#lem(label-name: "Lem_fil_cent_Wakimoto_unique")[
	Such a filtration is unique. 
] 
#proof[
	By @Lem_Wakimoto_hom_partial_ord, since the Ext groups of Wakimoto sheaves $RHom(J_lambda,J_mu)$ are non-zero only when $lambda<=mu$, upon fixing the total ordering compatible with the partial ordering and fixing the graded pieces, one can show that there is only one way to put the graded pieces together, and hence the filtration is unique. 
]

We now move on to prove the existence of the filtration. 

#def()[
	For $X in D_I (Fl)$, we set 
	$ W_X^*={w in W^ext|j_w^*(X)eq.not 0} $
	and 
	$ W_X^! ={w in W^ext|j_w^!(X)eq.not 0} $
]

#lem(label-name: "Lem_orbit_conv_estimate")[
	For $X in D_I$, there is a finite subset $S subset W^ext$, such that we have for all $w in W^ext$, we have
	$ W_(j_(w*)*_I X)^!,W_(j_(w!)*_I X)^* subset w dot S $
	and 
	$ W_(X*_I j_(w*))^!,W_(X*_I j_(w!))^* subset S dot w $
] 
#proof[
	By proper base change, the support of $j_(w!)*_I X$ is contained in the convolution of $Fl_w$ and $Supp X$. Hence to prove the assertion $W_(j_(w!)*_I X)^* subset w dot S$, it suffices to prove the corresponding assertion for the convolution of sets. Since $X$ is an $I$-equivariant sheaf, its support $frak(S)$ is an $I$-stable set. It suffices to show that there is some $S subset W^ext$, the convolution 
	$ Fl_w *_I frak(S) subset union.big.sq_(v in S dot w)Fl_v $
	By decomposing $frak(S)$ into $I$-orbits, the assertion follows from induction on the length of elements. The other three assertion is similar. 
]

#def(label-name: "Def_conv_exact_shf")[
	We define a perverse sheaf $cal(F) in Perv_I (Fl)$ to be convolution exact if $cal(F)*_I (-)$ is a functor from $Perv_I (Fl)->Perv_I (Fl)$. 
]

#prop()[
	Any convolution exact object in $Perv_I (Fl)$ has a Wakimoto filtration. If such object is central, only the Wakimoto sheaves $J_lambda$ for $lambda in X_*(T)$ will appear in the filtration. 
]
#proof[
	Let $X$ be convolution exact, and let $S$ be as in @Lem_orbit_conv_estimate. Then, writing the set $S={s_i}={lambda_i w_i}$ such that $lambda_i in X_*(T)$ and $w_i in W_f$, we choose $nu_0<<0 in X_*(T)$ such that $-(nu_0+lambda_i)$ is strictly dominant (that is, dominant and not lying in any wall). Thus, by a direct calculation, we have $J_(t_(-nu_0) s_i)=j_(t_(-nu_0) s_i !)$ by expanding the definition of Wakimoto sheaves. By @Lem_Wakimoto_perverse, we have 
	$ j_(t_(-nu_0) !)*_I X=J_(-nu_0)*_I X in Perv_I (Fl) $
	since $X$ is convolution exact. We use that this sheaf lies in the category $attach(D_I^(>=0) (Fl),tl:p)$. Then, by the gluing property of the perverse $t$-structure along closed and its complement open subschemes, we have by @Lem_orbit_conv_estimate 
	$ Supp (j_(t_(-nu_0) !)*_I X) subset union.big.sq_(s in S) Fl_(t_(-nu_0) s) $
	so we have $J_(nu_0)*_I X$ lie in the subcategory generated by $j_(t_(nu_0) dot s,!)[i]$ for $i>=0$ and $s in S$, since the sheaf is perverse and constructible with respect to this stratification. Then, by construction of $nu_0$, we have by definition $j_(t_(-nu_0) dot s,!)=J_(t_(-nu_0) dot s)$. To sum up, we have 
	$ J_(-nu_0) *_I X in Span {J_(-nu_0 dot s)[i] | i>=0,s in S} $
	and hence by $J_lambda *_I J_mu=J_(lambda+mu)$, 
	$ J_nu *_I X in Span {J_(t_nu dot s)[i] | i>=0,s in S} $
	for any $nu$. Then, choosing $nu$ a dominant coweight such that $nu+lambda_i$ are all dominant, we have 
	$ J_nu *_I X in Span {j_(t_nu dot s,*) | s in S} $
	where we do not need the shift because $J_nu *_I X$ is already perverse. This proves the existence of the filtration. 

	For the second part specifying the graded pieces in the filtration, we choose $lambda$ a dominant coweight such that $lambda dot S$ and $S dot lambda$ are all in $W_f$-orbits of strictly dominant coweights. By centrality, we have 
	$ W_(X*_I J_lambda)^! =W_(J_lambda *_I X)^! subset W_f dot X_*^(++)(T) inter X_*^(++)(T) dot W_f=X_*^(++)(T) $
	hence the claim. 
]

Now, by the results in the previous subsection, we have constructed the filtration. 

== The Iwahori–Whittaker sheaves

It turns out that we cannot directly prove the main equivalence. This is mainly because $D(attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f))!= attach(D_I (Fl),tl:f)$. Instead, we will first prove the following 

#thm([Theorem 3@AB], label-name: "Thm_3_Bez_Ark")[
	We have an equivalence of categories 
	$ Phi_IW: D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or)->D_IW (Fl) $
	where $D_IW (Fl)$ is a certain category of Iwahori–Whittaker sheaves that we will define below. 
]

and then relate the category of Iwahori–Whittaker sheaves to the original category. 

#thm([Theorem 2@AB], label-name: "Thm_2_Bez_Ark")[
	The averaging functor is an equivalence 
	$ Av_Psi:attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f)->^tilde.eq Perv_IW (Fl) $
]

Using the fact that $D^b (Perv_IW (Fl))tilde.eq D_IW (Fl)$ we will obtain the final result. 

The way of passing through the Iwahori–Whittaker sheaves is partially inspired by the method of Whittaker functions in automorphic theory. Moreover, these sheaves enjoy the following property. 

#prop([Lemma 1@AB])[
	By the formalism of highest weight categories, we have a natural equivalence 
	$ D_IW (Fl) = D(Perv_IW (Fl)) $
	and hence we have fully faithful maps 
	$ D_IW (Fl)subset D(Fl),Perv_IW (Fl)subset Perv(Fl) $
]

#def()[
	Consider $K' subset K$ algebraic groups acting on some $X$. Moreover suppose $K\/K'$ is of finite type. Then, we define functors on the category of equivariant sheaves (for any sheaf theory). 
	+	Let $res_(K')^K:Sh^K (X)->Sh^(K')(X)$ denote the natural forgetful functor, or the $*$-pullback functor of sheaves along the projection $X\/K'->X\/K$. 
	+	Its left adjoint $coind_(K')^K:Sh^(K')(X)->Sh^K (X)$. This is the shifted $!$-pushforward functor along the projection (since $pi$ is smooth). 
	+	Its right adjoint $ind_(K')^K:Sh^(K')(X)->Sh^K (X)$. This is the $*$-pushforward functor along the projection. 
]

#const(label-name: "Const_twisted_equi_shv")[
	Let $G$ be an algebraic group acting on $X$. Let $cal(L)$ be a character sheaf over $G$, that is, we have a morphism $m^*cal(L)tilde.eq cal(L)times.square cal(L)$, here $m$ stands for the multiplication map of the group $m:G times G->G$. 

	We define the category of $(G,cal(L))$-equivariant sheaf over $X$ to be defined via the following descent: 
	$ Sh^(G,cal(L))(X)=lim (Sh(X)arrows Sh(G times X)arrows.rrr Sh(G times G times X)dots.c) $
	where the morphisms in the limit diagram are given by either pulling back along the action map of $G$ on $X$ $a:G times X->X$, the multiplication map $m:G times G->G$, or by taking exterior tensor product with the character sheaf. 

	By definition, $Sh^G (X)=Sh^((G,cal(O)))(X)$. 

	Such construction is also compatible with the pro-group acting on ind-scheme formalism. 
]

#const()[
	We consider the negative Iwahori $I^-$. We denote its pro-unipotent radical by $I^-_0$. Also, consider the loop group of the negative unipotent subgroup $L N^-$. Consider the following additive character on $L N^-$: 
	$ L N^- ->N^- -> N^-\/[N^-,N^-]->^sum GG_a->AA^1 $
	where the first morphism is given by taking residues, the second is the natural projection, and the third is obtained by identifying $N^-\/[N^-,N^-]$ with a direct product of $GG_a$'s indexed by the simple roots and taking sums. This character induces a character $Psi:I_0->GG_a$ given by first restricting to the subgroup $I_0 inter N$ and then extending to the group $I_0$. 
	
	Then, since we are in characteristic $p$, consider the Artin-Schreier covering 
	$ A S=x^p-x:AA^1->>AA^1 $ 
	We have 
	$ A S_*(overline(QQ)_ell)=plus.o.big_(psi:FF_p->overline(QQ)_ell^times)cal(L)_psi $
	where $cal(L)_psi$ is a character sheaf over $GG_a=AA^1$ where the Galois group acts as $psi$. We fix any $psi$, and denote the corresponding $cal(L)_psi$ by $AS$. Now, pulling this sheaf back along the map $L N^- ->GG_a$ gives a character sheaf over $L N^-$. 
	
	We let $D_IW (Fl)$ and $Perv_IW (Fl)$ denote the corresponding $(I_0,Psi^*(AS))$-equivariant derived categories of $ell$-adic sheaves and perverse sheaves (using the pro-group acting on ind-schemes formalism as in the paper@AffGrsSat). Since $I_0$ is again contractible, and the $I_0^-$-orbits in $Fl$ are affine, $D_IW (Fl) subset D(Fl)$ is a full subcategory, and $D^b (Perv_IW (Fl))=D_IW (Fl)$. 
]

#const()[
	We now construct certain Iwahori–Whittaker perverse sheaves. Let $i_w:Fl^w->Fl$ denote the $I_0^-$-orbit in $Fl$ corresponding to $I_0^- w I$. We let $w in attach(W,tl:f)$ denote the minimal length element in the left coset $W_f w$. Then, for $w in attach(W,tl:f)$, we have $Stab_(I_0^-)(w I) subset ker Psi$. Hence, the definition $Psi_w:Fl^w->GG_a$ can be obtained by factoring $Psi:I_0^- ->GG_a$. Thus, we define for $w in attach(W,tl:f)$
	$ Delta_w=i_(w!)Psi_w^*(AS)[ell(w)],nabla_w=i_(w*)Psi_w^*(AS)[ell(w)] $
	We sometimes abuse notation and define $Delta_lambda:=Delta_w$ and $nabla_lambda:=nabla_w$, where $lambda in X_*(T)$, and $w$ is the element in $W^ext$ such that $w in W_f t_lambda subset W^ext$ is of minimal weight. 
	
	Then, we define $IC_w=i_(w!*)Psi_w^*(AS)[ell(w)]$. Since $Psi_w^*(AS)$ is an irreducible local system, $IC_w$ is a simple perverse sheaf viewed in $Perv(Fl)$. Clearly, these simple objects generate $Perv_IW (Fl)$. 
]

#const()[
	We may relate the category of Iwahori–Whittaker sheaves and the $I$-equivariant sheaves on the flag variety through the following averaging functor. We define $Av_Psi:D_I (Fl)->D_IW (Fl)$ by $cal(F) mapsto Delta_e *_I cal(F)$. Here $Av$ stands for averaging. $Delta_e *_I cal(F)$ is indeed an Iwahori–Whittaker sheaf, since pullbacks commute with convolution, $I_0^-$ is conjugate to $I_0$ by $w_0$, $cal(F)$ is $I_0$-invariant, and $Delta_e$ transforms by tensoring with the character sheaf. 
]

We now present some basic properties of the functor $Av_Psi$. 

#prop(label-name: "Prop_basic_avr")[
	+	$Av_Psi (L_w)eq.not 0$ iff $w in attach(W,tl:f)$. 
	+	$Delta_e=nabla_e$. 
	+	For any $w in W$, we factor it as $w_f dot w'$, where $w_f in W_f$ and $w' in attach(W,tl:f)$. Then, we have #h(1fr)
		$ Av_Psi (j_(w!))=Delta_(w'),Av_Psi (j_(w*))=nabla_(w') $
]
#proof[
	+	If $w in attach(W,tl:f)$, then the support of $Delta_e times.square_I L_w$ is $I_0^- I times^I overline(Fl)_w$. Notice that $I_0^- I=I_0 w_0 I=I w_0 I$, so by the minimality of the length of $w$, we have the convolution map $Fl times^I Fl->Fl$ the identity on the generic point of the support of $Delta_e times.square_I L_w$. Thus, $Delta_e *_I L_w eq.not 0$. Then for the converse, if the length of $w$ is not minimal in $W_f dot.c w$, say $s w<=w$ for some simple reflection, then  we have $L_w$ equivariant with respect to some minimal parahoric $I_s$. Also, the functor $Delta_e*_I (-):D_(I_s)(Fl)->D_(IW)(Fl)$ factors through the projection $pi_(s*)$, where $pi_s:L G\/I->>L G\/I_s$ is the projection. But notice $pi_(s*)(Delta_e)=0$ since the character $Psi$ is non-trivial on the image of the $I w_0 I$, so we are done. 
	+	Firstly, notice that $Psi$ is non-vanishing on any point of 
		$ overline(Fl)^e-Fl^e=union.big_(w<=w_0)I w I $
		Thus $Delta_e=nabla_e$ are both the constant sheaves supported on $Fl^e$. 
	+	It suffices to prove $Av_Psi (j_(w!))=Delta_(w')$, since the other one follows from duality. Firstly, if already we have $w in attach(W,tl:f)$, the assertion is trivial since analogous to (1), we have the comparison map an equivalence on $Fl^w$, and analogous to (2), we have LHS vanishes outside $Fl^w$ (RHS obviously vanishes outside $Fl^w$). For general $w$, the formula follows from 
		$ Delta_e*_I j_(w!)=Delta_e *_I j_(w_f !)*_I j_(w'!)=(Delta_e*_I j_(w_f !))*_I j_(w'!)=Delta_e *_I j_(w'!)=Delta_(w'!) $ 
		where the first equality is by @Lem_conv_I_equi_shv, the second by the associativity of convolution, and the third follows from @Lem_num_id_I_equi. 
] 

It turns out that $Av_Psi$ is an equivalence of categories $attach(cal(P),tl:f)->Perv_IW (Fl)$. However, we will not be able to prove this for now. Instead, we will prove the following proposition and delay the proof of the above fact until the end of the proof of the main theorem @Thm_main_Bez_Ark. 

#prop(label-name: "Prop_comp_I_IW_1st_half")[
	+	We have the functor $Av_Psi:D_I (Fl)->D_(IW)(Fl)$ restricts to a functor $Perv_I (Fl)->Perv_(IW)(Fl)$. 
	+	This functor factors through the quotient $Perv_I (Fl)->>attach(cal(P),tl:f)$. 
	+	The induced functor $attach(cal(P),tl:f)-> Perv_(IW)(Fl)$ is fully faithful. 
] 

To prove this, we construct its right inverse. 

#const()[
	We first wish to construct a functor $Av_I:D_(IW)(Fl)->D_I (Fl)$. However, since $D_(IW)(Fl)$ is a twisted equivariant derived category, direct induction does not seem to work. In the setting here, we are lucky enough: we have $I_0^- inter I=t L^+ G$, and the residue map $L N^- ->N$ is obviously trivial on $L N^- inter t L^+G$. Hence, the restriction to $I_0^- inter I=t L^+G$ gives a non-twisted $t L^+G$-equivariant sheaf. We may define $Av_I$ to be $ind_(t L^+ G)^I res_(t L^+G)^(I_0^-)$. 

	Then, to obtain an inverse on the level of perverse sheaves, we consider the composition 
	$ Perv_IW (Fl)-->^(Av_I)D_I (Fl)-->^(attach(H,tl:p,tr:ell(w_0)))Perv_I (Fl)->>attach(cal(P),tl:f) $
	where $w_0$ is the longest element in the finite Weyl group. We denote this functor by $F$ (since we will not really use this functor after the proof of @Prop_comp_I_IW_1st_half). 
]

#lem()[
	We have $F compose Av_Psi=id$ restricted to $attach(cal(P),tl:f)$. 
]
#proof[
	We wish to compute for $cal(F) in Perv_I (Fl)$ the object $F compose Av_Psi (attach(cal(F),tl:f))$. Expanding definitions, we have 
	$ F compose Av_Psi (attach(cal(F),tl:f))=attach((attach(H,tl:p,tr:ell(w_0))(Av_I (Delta_e *_I attach(cal(F),tl:f)))),tl:f) $
	We break this series of functors from inside. 

	We have first 
	$ Av_I (Delta_e *_I attach(cal(F),tl:f))=attach((Av_I (Delta_e)*_I cal(F)),tl:f) $
	this is because projecting to $attach(cal(P),tl:f)$ commutes with everything, and left Iwahori averaging by construction commutes with convolution on the right. 

	Then, we must compute the cohomology 
	$ attach(H^(ell(w_0)),tl:p)(attach((Av_I (Delta_e)*_I cal(F)),tl:f))=attach((attach(H^0,tl:p)(Av_I (Delta_e [ell(w_0)])*_I cal(F))),tl:f) $
	where we put the cohomology index inside, and move the projection outside. We first compute $attach(H^0,tl:p)(Av_I (Delta_e[ell(w_0)]))$. We first construct a morphism $j_(w_0 !)->attach(H^0,tl:p)(Av_I (Delta_e[ell(w_0)]))$ with cofiber lying in $attach(D^(>0)_I,tl:p)$. 

	For this morphism, note 
	$ Hom_(D_I (Fl))(X,Av_I (Delta_e)[ell(w_0)])=Hom_D(Fl)(X,Delta_e [ell(w_0)]) $
	For the right hand side, note $D_(t L^+G)(Fl)subset D(Fl)$ is a full subcategory since $t L^+G$ is unipotent (contractible), and the sheaves should lie in $D_(t L^+G)(Fl)$. Then, the proof of @Prop_basic_avr gives $RHom(L_w,Delta_e)=0$ for all $e!=w in W_f$, and $Hom(delta_e,Delta_e [ell(w_0)])=overline(QQ)_ell$. Hence by @Lem_num_id_I_equi, note that $j_(w!)$ (for $w in W_f$) contains only $L_(w')$ ($w' in W_f$ also) in its composition series and exactly one $delta_e$, so we have 
	$ Hom_(D_I)(j_(w!),Av_I (Delta_e)[ell(w_0)])=overline(QQ)_ell $ 
	and for $w<w'$, the composition of the inclusion with the unique non-zero map 
	$ j_(w!)inj j_(w'!)->Av_I (Delta_e)[ell(w_0)] $ 
	is also non-zero again by @Lem_num_id_I_equi. Hence, the cofiber $C$ of $j_(w_0!)->Av_I (Delta_e)[ell(w_0)]$ has the property that for every $w in W_f$, $Hom(j_(w!),C[i])=0$ for $i<=0$. Moreover, since $Av_I (Delta_e)$ is supported on the finite part $I W_f I$, we have $C in attach(D^(>0),tl:p)$. Moreover, the map $j_(w_0 !)->attach(H^0,tl:p)(Av_I (Delta_e)[ell(w_0)])$ is an isomorphism in the category $Perv_I (Fl)$ since its kernel must be of the form $j_(w!)$ for some $w in W_f$, which is not possible since 
	$ j_(w!)inj j_(w_0 !)->Av_I (Delta_e)[ell(w_0)] $
	is non-zero. 

	Hence, we have finished the following reduction. 
	$ attach((attach(H^0,tl:p)(Av_I (Delta_e [ell(w_0)])*_I cal(F))),tl:f)=attach((attach(H^0,tl:p)(j_(w_0 !)*_I cal(F))),tl:f) $
	Finally, since $attach(L_w,tl:f)=0$ for all $e!=w in W_f$, we have the final perverse sheaf $attach(cal(F),tl:f)$ itself. 

	Hence $F$ is indeed a right inverse! 
]

#proof[
	We now prove @Prop_comp_I_IW_1st_half. 
	+	To prove that $Av_Psi$ restricts to a functor of perverse sheaves, we need the following characterization (by the gluing definition of perverse t-structures) of their perverse $t$-heart. Namely, we have 
		$ Perv_I (Fl)=Span{j_(w!)[i],i>=0}inter Span{j_(w*)[i],i<=0} $
		and 
		$ Perv_IW (Fl)=Span{Delta_(w!)[i],i>=0}inter Span{nabla_(w*)[i],i<=0} $
		However, we know by @Prop_basic_avr(3) that $Av_Psi$ preserves exactly these objects. Hence $Av_Psi$ restricts to a functor of the categories of perverse sheaves. 
	+	This is by the universal property of Serre quotients and @Prop_basic_avr(1). 
	+	With the previous lemma, we are very close to full faithfulness. For this, we need the following claim on Abelian categories: if a functor $F:cal(A)->cal(B)$ of Abelian categories satisfies the following list of properties 
		+	$F$ is exact. #h(1fr)
		+	Every object of $cal(A)$ is of finite length. 
		+	$F$ induces an isomorphism on the Hom groups 
			$ Hom_cal(A)(L_1,L_2)->^tilde.eq Hom_cal(B)(F(L_1),F(L_2)) $
			for all irreducible objects $L_i in cal(A)$. 
		+	$F$ has a right inverse. 
		then it is fully faithful. This is a simple fact obtained by applying induction on the length of $A,B in cal(A)$ to prove $Hom_cal(A)(A,B)->^tilde.eq Hom_cal(B)(F(A),F(B))$. 

		Now, we apply this claim to the functor $Av_Psi$. Note that (1) provides that $Av_Psi$ is t-exact, so we have $Av_Psi$ exact after restricting to the category of perverse sheaves. (2) is naturally satisfied by the category of perverse sheaves. Then, note 
		$ Av_Psi (L_w)=Av_Psi (im(j_(w!)->j_(w*)))=im(Delta_(w)->nabla_w) $ 
		are simple, and these objects are distinct since they have different supports. Thus by the property of morphism of simple objects, (3) is satisfied. (4) is verified in the previous lemma, so we are done. 
]

== Hecke algebras 

In this subsection, we identify the $K^0$-groups of certain sheaf categories, allowing us to explicitly handle the computations of some Euler characteristics, for example in @Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_1. 

#def[
	Recall that for a reductive group $G$, we define its affine Hecke algebra $cal(H)$ to be generated by the following objects and relations: 
	+	Let $cal(H)$ be a $ZZ[v,v^(-1)]$-algebra with generators $H_w$ for $w in W^ext$. 
	+	The relations are given by $(H_s+v)(H_s-v^(-1))=0$ for $s in S$ a simple reflection, and $H_u H_v=H_(u v)$ for $ell(u)+ell(v)=ell(u v)$. 
	We let $cal(M)^sph=cal(H)times.o_(cal(H)_f)triv$, where $cal(H)_f$ is the finite Weyl group. 
]

Recall that the Hecke algebra admits a Kazhdan-Lusztig basis $B_w$. Clearly, we have $ZZ times.o_(ZZ[v,v^(-1)])cal(H)=ZZ[W]$. We will also denote the basis of this algebra by $H_w$. 

#prop(label-name: "Prop_K0_Iwahori_Fl")[
	We have $ZZ[W]=K^0(D_I (Fl))$, where the morphism is given by $(-1)^(ell(w))H_w mapsto [j_(w*)]$. Moreover, $(-1)^(ell(w))B_w mapsto [L_(w)]$. 
]
#proof[
	First, note $j_(w*)$ generates the category $D_I (Fl)$, so this morphism is surjective. Then, since these sheaves have different supports, taking the homology of the stalks gives a function 
	$ phi_w:K^0(D_I (Fl))->ZZ, phi_w ([cal(F)])=sum_(n in ZZ)(-1)^n dim H^n (Fl_(w),j_w^*cal(F)) $
	satisfies $phi_w ([j_(u*)])=(-1)^ell(w)delta_(w,u)$, so these $[j_(w*)]$ are all linearly independent. Hence, the map is injective. 
	
	Thus, the map is a bijection of Abelian groups, and it now suffices to show that it is a morphism of rings. But this is equivalent to 
	$ [j_(u*)*_I j_(v*)]=[j_(u*)][j_(v*)] $
	This is true because for $ell(v)=0$, or $ell(u v)=ell(u)+ell(v)$, the statement for underlying sheaf holds by @Lem_conv_I_equi_shv. If $ell(v)=1$, $ell(u v)=ell(u)-1$, so since $v$ is a simple reflection, we have 
	$ [j_(u v*)][j_(v*)]=[j_(u*)] $
	and since the simple perverse sheaves are preserved under Verdier duality, we have, by explicitly writing out the exact sequence on $PP^1tilde.eq Fl_w$, 
	$ [j_(v*)]=[j_(v!)] $
	Hence, 
	$ [j_(u v*)]=[j_(u v*)][j_(v*)][j_(v!)]=[j_(u*)][j_(v!)]=[j_(u*)][j_(v*)] $
	The part on Kazhdan-Lusztig basis is standard. 
]

Then, we relate this construction to the $K$-group of Affine grassmannians. 

#prop[
	We have an isomorphism 
	$ ZZ times.o_(ZZ[v,v^(-1)])cal(M)^sph=K^0(D_I (Gr)) $
	such that the projection $cal(H)->>cal(M)^sph$ corresponds to $pi_*:Fl->>Gr$. 
]
#proof[
	Likewise as in the above. 
]

#def[
	We define $theta_lambda=[J_lambda] in H[W]$ to be the class representing the Wakimoto sheaves. We may upgrade this construction to the Hecke algebra by taking $theta_lambda=H_mu H_nu^(-1)$ for $mu-nu=lambda$ and $mu,nu in X_*(T)^+$. 
]

#prop(label-name: "Prop_compute_mult_Waki_K")[
	For any $V in Rep G^or$, we have 
	$ [Z(V)]=sum_(lambda in X_*(T))dim V^(T^or=lambda) dot theta_lambda in K^0(D_I (Fl)) $
]
#proof[
	The proof of this proposition relies on @Prop_waki_fil_gr_eq_res. The rest follows from the Wakimoto filtration. 
]

Then, to give a more delicate characterization of the nilpotent endomorphism $M_V$, we give another calculation (though standard) on the Hecke algebras and $K$-groups. 

#prop(label-name: "Prop_Hecke_alg_eq_K_tensor")[
	We have an isomorphism 
	$ R times.o_(ZZ[v,v^(-1)])cal(H)=K^0(D_(I,mixed)(Fl)) $
	sending $1 times.o H_w mapsto (-1)^(ell(w))[j_(w*)^m]$ and $v mapsto [overline(QQ)_ell (-1\/2)]$. Here $R=K^0(D_mixed (Spec FF_q))$. 

	Finally, we still have $1 times.o B_w mapsto (-1)^(ell(w))[L_w^m]$. 
]
#proof[
	Again this morphism is surjective since the standard objects generate the $K$-group under $R$, and injective since we may again take the stalks at each point 
	$ phi_w:K^0(D_I (Fl))->R, phi_w ([cal(F)])=[j_w^*(cal(F))]in K^0(D_mixed (Spec FF_q)) $
	where we may regard $[j_w^*(cal(F))]$ as a mixed sheaf on a point since it is a constant sheaf, so it is an isomorphism of $R$-module. 

	Now, for this to be an algebra morphism, we need
	$ [j_(u*)^m *_I j_(v*)^m] = [j_(u v*)^m] $
	for $ell(u)+ell(v)=ell(u v)$. This holds by a mixed version of @Lem_conv_I_equi_shv. Finally, we still need 
	$ ([j_(u*)^m]+[overline(QQ)_ell (-1/2)])([j_(u*)^m]-[overline(QQ)_ell (1/2)])=0 $
	This follows from the exact triangle over $PP^1=overline(Fl_s)$ ($i:oo inj PP^1$, $j:AA^1 inj PP^1$)
	$ i_*i^!overline(QQ)_ell [1](1/2)->overline(QQ)_ell [1](1/2)->j_*j^* overline(QQ)_ell [1](1/2) $
	Note $j_*j^* overline(QQ)_ell [1](1\/2)=j_(s*)^m$. Then, note 
	$ 
	i^! overline(QQ)_ell [1](1/2)=&DD i^* DD overline(QQ)_ell [1](1/2) \
	=&DD i^* overline(QQ)_ell [1](1/2) \
	=&DD overline(QQ)_ell [1](1/2) \
	=&overline(QQ)_ell [-1](-1/2)
	$
	Thus we arrive at a triangle 
	$ L_s^m->j_(s*)^m->L_e^m (-1/2) $
	which gives the relation 
	$ [j_(s*)^m]=[L_s^m]+v $
	so 
	$ (H_s+v)(H_s-v^(-1))=[L_s^m]([L_s^m]+v+v^(-1)) $
	but by an explicit computation of the convolution $L_s^m *_I L_s^m$ on $S L_2$, we have the right hand side hold. 
]

#prop[
	Again, we have 
	$ R times.o_(ZZ[v,v^(-1)])cal(M)^sph = K^0(D_I (Gr)) $
	with the projection $cal(H)->>cal(M)^sph$ corresponding to $pi_*$. 
]
#proof[
	Likewise as above. 
]

#prop(label-name: "Prop_num_Waki_wt_fil")[
	For any $lambda in X_*(T)^+$, the class $[Z^m (V_lambda)]in R times.o_(ZZ[v,v^(-1)])cal(H)$ is given by 
	$ sum_(mu in X_*(T))dim V_lambda^(T^or=mu) dot theta_mu^m $
	where $theta_lambda^m$ is the element in the $K$-group represented by $[J_lambda^m]$, and $J_lambda^m=j_(mu*)^m *_I j_(nu!)^m$ is the mixed version of the Wakimoto sheaf. 
]
#proof[
	Let 
	$ Q=sum_(w in W_f)v^(2 ell(w)) $
	This time, counting in the weights, by the projection formula we have 
	$ [pi_*pi^* j_(lambda!*)^(m,Gr)]=Q[j_(lambda!*)^(m,Gr)] $
	Then, since we have $pi^*j_(lambda!*)^(m,Gr)$ is simple since $pi$ is smooth, we have this sheaf of the form $j_(w!*)^m$ for some $w in W^ext$. Then, we can obviously identify $w$ as the maximal length element with $Fl_w$ mapping in $Gr_lambda$, which we will denote by $n_lambda$. Thus, $pi^*j_(lambda!*)^(m,Gr)=j_(n_lambda !*)^m$. 

	Then, note $pi:Fl_(n_lambda)->>Gr_(lambda)$ is an affine fibration of dimension $ell(w_0)$. Thus, we may compute 
	$ [pi_*pi^*j_(lambda!*)^(m,Gr)]=(-v)^(ell(w_0))[pi_*j_(n_lambda !*)^m] $
	Thus, we have 
	$ [j_(lambda!*)^(m,Gr)]=1/Q (-1)^(ell(n_lambda)-ell(w_0))v^(ell(w_0))B_n_lambda in R times.o_(ZZ[v,v^(-1)])cal(M)^sph $
	But by Kazhdan-Lusztig theory we have 
	$ B_(n_lambda)=(-1)^(ell(n_lambda)-ell(w_0))B_(w_0)(sum_(mu in X_*(T))dim V_lambda^(T^or=mu)dot theta_mu^m) $
	But note in $cal(M)^sph$, we have $Q=v^(ell(w_0))B_(w_0)$ by a standard computation of $L_(w_0)$. Hence the result follows from that $Z(cal(H))->cal(H)->cal(M)^sph$ is injective, which is well-known. 
]

= Constructing and proving the equivalence 

Now, we move on to construct the functor and proving the equivalence. 

== The de-equivariantization principle 

In this subsection, following Achar–Riche@CentralShvAR, we will state the de-equivariantization principle (originally developed by Bezrukavnikov in his work). This will allow us to build up the desired equivalence step by step. 

#rem()[
	In short, the de-equivariantization principle answers the following question. Given a reductive group $H$, a commutative ring $A$ equipped with an $H$-action, we may define the category $Mod_(A,fr)^H$ with objects given by the finite free $H$-equivariant $A$-modules (explicitly, modules of the form $V times.o A^n$ where $V in Rep H$), and morphisms the $H$-equivariant morphisms. Now, suppose we have another monoidal Abelian category $cal(A)$ and a monoidal functor $Rep H->cal(A)$, when can we lift this functor to a monoidal $Mod_(A,fr)^H->cal(A)$? We will first display a toy example that will turn out to be much more useful than it seem. 
]

#const(label-name: "Const_deequi_module")[
	We first take $cal(A)=Mod_B^K$, where $K subset H$ is a subgroup acting on a ring $B$. Then, we have a symmetric monoidal functor $Rep H->Mod_B^K$ given by $V mapsto V|_K times.o B$. We claim that an upgrade of this functor to a symmetric monoidal $Mod_A^H->Mod_(B)^K$ for some $H$-equivariant ring $A$ is equivalent to a $K$-equivariant morphism $A->B$. 

	For one, such morphism clearly gives rise to the tensor functor $M mapsto M times.o_A B$. For the other, suppose such a lift exist, recall that by the Peter-Weyl theorem we have 
	$ cal(O)(H)=plus.o.big_(lambda in X^*(T)^+)V_lambda times.o V_lambda^* in Ind(Rep H) $
	By definition, $H$-equivariant module is an $cal(O)(H)$-comodule, where the coalgebra structure of $cal(O)(H)$ comes from the group structure of $H$. Thus, this object satisfies the universal property that 
	$ Hom_(Ind(Mod_A^H))(M,cal(O)(H)times.o N)tilde.eq Hom_(Mod_A)(M,N) $
	and in particular we have 
	$ Hom_(Ind(Mod_A^H))(A,cal(O)(H)times.o A)=A $
	where this isomorphism respects the algebra structure of $A$. 

	Thus, a symmetric monoidal functor $Mod_A^H->Mod_B^K$ asserts firstly that $A mapsto B$, so upon ind-completing the functor, it induces a morphism 
	$ Hom_(Ind(Mod_A^H))(A,cal(O)(H)times.o A)->Hom_(Ind(Mod_B^K))(B,cal(O)(H)times.o A) $
	On the other hand, the right hand side Hom can be identified with $(cal(O)(H)times.o A)^K$, so we obtain an equivariant map $A->B$ by 
	$ 
	A=&Hom_(Ind(Mod_A^H))(A,cal(O)(H)times.o A) \
	->&Hom_(Ind(Mod_B^K))(B,cal(O)(H)times.o B) \
	=&(cal(O)(H)times.o B)^K \
	->&(cal(O)(K)times.o B)^K=B
	$
	the two constructions are evidently inverse to each other. 
]

#const(label-name: "Const_full_vers_deeq")[
	We then treat the general case where we wish to find the monoidal lift $tilde(F)$ of the symmetric monoidal functor $F$ // https://q.uiver.app/#r=typst&q=WzAsMyxbMCwwLCJSZXAoSCkiXSxbMCwxLCJNb2RfKEEsZnIpXkgiXSxbMSwwLCJjYWwoQSkiXSxbMCwxXSxbMCwyLCJGIl0sWzEsMiwidGlsZGUoRikiLDIseyJzdHlsZSI6eyJib2R5Ijp7Im5hbWUiOiJkYXNoZWQifX19XV0=
	#align(center, diagram({
		node((0, 0), [$Rep H$])
		node((0, 1), [$Mod_(A,fr)^H$])
		node((1, 0), [$cal(A)$])
		edge((0, 0), (0, 1), "->")
		edge((0, 0), (1, 0), [$F$], label-side: left, "->")
		edge((0, 1), (1, 0), [$tilde(F)$], label-side: right, "-->")
	}))
	where $cal(A)$ is assumed merely to be monoidal. The method is to reduce to the previous case. Since $Rep(H)$ is symmetric monoidal, the commutativity constraint $V_1 times.o V_2tilde.eq V_2 times.o V_1$ is sent by $F$ to a commutativity constraint $F(V_1)times.o F(V_2)tilde.eq F(V_2)times.o F(V_1)$. Then, we construct a faithful functor of monoidal Abelian categories of $cal(C) subset cal(A)$. We define $cal(C)$ to be the category with objects the same as $Rep H$, and for objects $V,V'$, we define $Hom_cal(C)(V,V')subset Hom_cal(A)(F(V),F(V'))$ to be the subgroup of morphisms $f$ such that for any object $V''$ the following diagram commutes // https://q.uiver.app/#r=typst&q=WzAsNCxbMCwwLCJGKFYnJyl0aW1lcy5vIEYoVikiXSxbMCwxLCJGKFYpdGltZXMubyBGKFYnJykiXSxbMSwwLCJGKFYnJyl0aW1lcy5vIEYoVicpIl0sWzEsMSwiRihWJyl0aW1lcy5vIEYoVicnKSJdLFswLDIsImlkIHRpbWVzLm8gZiJdLFswLDEsInRpbGRlLmVxIiwyXSxbMiwzLCJ0aWxkZS5lcSJdLFsxLDMsImYgdGltZXMubyBpZCJdXQ==
	#align(center, diagram({
		node((0, 0), [$F(V'')times.o F(V)$])
		node((0, 1), [$F(V)times.o F(V'')$])
		node((1, 0), [$F(V'')times.o F(V')$])
		node((1, 1), [$F(V')times.o F(V'')$])
		edge((0, 0), (1, 0), [$id times.o f$], label-side: left, "->")
		edge((0, 0), (0, 1), [$tilde.eq$], label-side: right, "->")
		edge((1, 0), (1, 1), [$tilde.eq$], label-side: left, "->")
		edge((0, 1), (1, 1), [$f times.o id$], label-side: left, "->")
	}))

	We first attempt to identify this category $cal(C)$. Notice that $cal(C)$ inherits the monoidal structure from $cal(A)$, and moreover the above commutative square makes $cal(C)$ a symmetric monoidal category. We claim that $cal(C)$ can be identified as a symmetric monoidal category with $Mod_(B,fr)^H$ for some $B$. Firstly, we have the symmetric monoidal functor $F:Rep(H)->cal(A)$ factors through the inclusion $cal(C)inj cal(A)$, in which we denote the factoring by $F_cal(C):Rep H->cal(C)$. We define 
	$ B=Hom_(Ind(cal(C)))(1_(cal(C)),F_cal(C)(cal(O)(H))) $
	Then, we may construct a functor $H:cal(C)->Mod_(B,fr)^H$ given by 
	$ H(V):=Hom_(Ind(cal(C)))(1_(cal(C)),F_cal(C)(V)times.o F_cal(C)(cal(O)(H))) $
	By moving $F_cal(C)(V)times.o F_cal(C)(cal(O)_H (H))=F_cal(C)(V times.o cal(O)(H))$, we may twist the $H$-action on $V$ into $cal(O)(H)$ through an isomorphism of $H$-modules. Thus, we may assume $V$ is a trivial $H$-module, and we move $V$ to the outside and we may identify $H(V)=V times.o B$ (as a direct corollary, $H$ is essentially surjective). With the same process, we see that $H$ is fully faithful. By unwinding the definitions, we see $H$ is monoidal. 

	Hence, we have the data of a lift $tilde(F)$ coming from an $H$-equivariant map $A->B$ for $B$ defined above.  
]

#rem[
	Actually, the full content of the de-equivariantization principle extends further. Recall that we may define for a ring $A$ the concept of $A$-linear categories. We may extend this definition to $cal(X)$-linear categories, where $cal(X)$ is a general stack. Notice that a monoidal functor of monoidal categories $Rep H->cal(A)$ is equivalent to a $BB H$-linear structure on $cal(A)$ (here $BB H$ is the Artin stack of the delooping of $H$). Then, an extension to $Mod_(A,fr)^H->cal(A)$ is equivalent to a $((Spec A)\/H)$-linear structure on $cal(A)$. 
	
	So, one may expect that the de-equivariantization principle would provide the equivalent piece of data of upgrading a $BB H$-linear category into a $((Spec A)\/H)$-linear category. Explicitly, this piece of data is an $H$-equivariant $A$-linear structure on $cal(A)_(upright("deeq"))$, where $cal(A)_(upright("deeq"))$ is the $cal(O)(H)$-algebras in $cal(A)$. 
]

== The construction of the functor $Phi$

We first give a brief outline of the construction of the equivalence $Phi$ (the functor in @Thm_main_Bez_Ark). 

#const(label-name: "Const_main_equiv_fct")[
	We wish to construct a functor originating from $D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(N)^or)$. Recall that the Springer resolution $tilde(N)^or$ on the dual side is given by 
	$ cal(B)^or times frak(g)^or supset tilde(N)^or={(frak(b),x)in B^or times frak(g)^or|x in frak(b)} $
	However, $tilde(N)^or$ is the total space of a vector bundle over a projective space, hence highly projective in nature. This blocks our way to analyse the structure of its category of coherent sheaves directly. 

	The way to get past this is considering the $T^or$-torsor $G^or\/U^or->>cal(B)^or$. It is well-known that $G^or\/U^or$ is quasi-affine. By the Peter-Weyl theorem, the affine closure of $G^or\/U^or$ is given by 
	$ overline(G^or\/U^or)=Spec cal(O)(G^or\/U^or)=Spec(plus.o.big_(lambda in X_*(T)^+)V_lambda) $
	Here, we give a commutative ring structure on $plus.o.big V_lambda$. Firstly, the underlying $k$-vector space structure of this ring is the direct sum of all finite dimensional $G^or$-representations. Then, notice that by the formalism of highest weight representations, we have a non-trivial morphism (up to scalar, so we fix one compatible with each other) 
	$ m_(lambda+rho):V_lambda times.o V_rho->V_(lambda+rho) $
	which is defined as follows: notice that the highest weight line $b_lambda subset V_lambda$ and the highest weight line $b_rho subset V_rho$ gives a highest weight line $b_lambda times.o b_rho subset V_lambda times.o V_rho$, and hence a factor $V_(lambda+rho)inj V_lambda times.o V_rho$. Since the category of $G^or$-representations are semisimple, we get a projection $V_lambda times.o V_rho->V_(lambda+rho)$. 

	We first let the preimage of $tilde(cal(N))^or$ in $G^or\/U^or times frak(g)^or$ be $hat(cal(N))^or$. Then, since $hat(cal(N))^or$ is closed in $G^or\/U^or times frak(g)^or$, consider the ideal sheaf associated to it, say $cal(I) subset cal(O)_(G^or\/U^or times frak(g)^or)$. Then, $cal(I)(G^or\/U^or times frak(g)^or)subset cal(O)(G^or\/U^or times frak(g)^or)$ is an ideal, which gives rise to a closed subscheme of $overline(G^or\/U^or) times frak(g)^or$, which we call $hat(cal(N))^or_aff$. Clearly, we have 
	$ hat(cal(N))^or_aff times_(overline(G^or\/U^or)times frak(g)^or)(G^or\/U^or times frak(g)^or) = hat(cal(N))^or $
	and the inclusion $hat(cal(N))^or subset hat(cal(N))^or_aff$ exhibits $hat(cal(N))^or$ as a quasi-affine scheme. We denote its boundary by $partial hat(cal(N))^or:=hat(cal(N))^or_aff-hat(cal(N))^or$. 

	On the other hand, the category of perverse sheaves $Perv_I (Fl)$ is not monoidal, since convolution takes objects out. We must replace it by the monidal category $Perv_I^waki (Fl)$, which is the subcategory of $Perv_I (Fl)$ consisting of objects admitting a filtration by Wakimoto sheaves. This category is monoidal since $J_lambda *_I J_mu=J_(lambda+mu)$, and it is still large enough to contain the image of the central functor $Z$. 

	With the above definitions, our way to define the functor $Phi$ can be summarized as follows: 
	+	Find a monoidal functor #h(1fr)
		$ Phi_0:Rep(G^or times T^or)->Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
		and find the factoring through the faithful subcategory $cal(C)subset Perv_I^waki (Fl)$ (constructed in the previous section, proven to be equivalent to a module category), whose induced functor $Rep(G^or times T^or)->cal(C)$ is symmetric monoidal. 
	+	Extend it via the de-equivariantization principle to 
		$ Phi_1:Coh_fr^(G^or times T^or)(overline(G^or\/U^or) times frak(g)^or)->cal(C)inj Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
	+	Restrict again via the de-equivariantization principle to 
		$ Phi_2:Coh_fr^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)->cal(C)inj Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
	+	Post-compose the inclusion $Perv_I^waki (Fl)inj Perv_I (Fl)$, and take homotopy categories. We have by definition 
		$ K(Coh_fr^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff))=Perf^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff) $
		and for the regular stack $hat(cal(N))^or\/(G^or times T^or)$, we have 
		$ Perf^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)=D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff) $
		Hence we arrive at a map 
		$ K(Phi_2):D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)->K(Perv_I (Fl))->>D(Perv_I (Fl)) $
		We then show the complexes of sheaves supported on $partial hat(cal(N))^or$ are sent by $Phi_3$ to acyclic complexes, so we obtain a functor 
		$ 
		Phi_3:D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)\/D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)_(partial hat(cal(N))^or)=&D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or)\
		->&D(Perv_I (Fl)) 
		$
		But we have by definition 
		$ D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or)=D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or) $
		Then by projecting to $attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f)$, the construction of $Phi$ is done, although we will more often use the functor with image $D(Perv_I (Fl))$ rather than the one with image $D(attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f))$. 
]

We then exhibit the four steps stated above more clearly. 

In these steps, we make more use of the Wakimoto sheaves, especially the Wakimoto filtrations, proven to exist for central convolution exact sheaves, for example the image of $Z$, as in @Prop_fil_cent_Wakimoto. 

#def[
	Firstly, we let $Perv_I^waki (Fl)$ denote the subcategory of $Perv_I (Fl)$ consisting of objects admitting a filtration by Wakimoto sheaves. By @Lem_fil_cent_Wakimoto_unique, the filtration on each $cal(F) in Perv_I^waki (Fl)$ is unique, so we may define an associated graded functor 
	$ gr_waki:Perv_I^waki (Fl)->Rep T^or $
	since we recall that the Wakimoto sheaves are indexed by coweights. Moreover, since $J_lambda *_I J_mu=J_(lambda+mu)$, so $Perv_I^waki (Fl) subset D_I (Fl)$ inherits the monoidal structure. 
]

#const(label-name: "Const_main_equiv_fct_step0")[
	We construct the functor 
	$ Phi_0:Rep(G^or times T^or)->Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
	Recall in @Thm_central1 and @Prop_fil_cent_Wakimoto we have constructed a functor $Z:Perv_(L^+G)(Gr)->Perv^waki_I (Fl)$. Precomposing this with the Satake functor gives 
	$ Rep G^or-->^(Sat_G)Perv_(L^+G)(Gr)-->^Z Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
	The Satake functor is symmetric monoidal and the central functor is central and monoidal, so this is monoidal. We write $Z(Sat_G (V_lambda)):=Z_lambda$ for short. On the other hand, by @Lem_Waki_sym_mon_RepT, we have the Wakimoto sheaves functor 
	$ Rep T^or->Perv^waki_I (Fl), (overline(QQ)_ell)_lambda mapsto J_lambda $
	is monoidal. Thus, the two functors combines and give a functor 
	$ Rep (G^or times T^or)->Perv^waki_I (Fl), V_lambda times.o (overline(QQ)_ell)_eta mapsto Z_lambda*_I J_eta $
	This functor is monoidal since $Z$ is in addition central. 
]

#const(label-name: "Const_main_equiv_fct_step1")[
	We now construct the functor 
	$ Phi_1:Coh_fr^(G^or times T^or)(overline(G^or\/U^or) times frak(g)^or)->Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
	Here, we first specify the $(G^or times T^or)$-action on $overline(G^or\/U^or) times frak(g)^or$. The torus $T^or$ acts on $overline(G^or\/U^or)$ by multiplying on the right, and on $frak(g)^or$ trivially. The group $G^or$ acts on $overline(G^or\/U^or)$ on the left (or, equivalently, the resulting action on the $G^or\/B^or$-part of the Springer resolution is given by $g dot frak(b)^or=Ad_g frak(b)^or$), and the adjoint action on $frak(g)^or$. 

	Again, we construct the two functors separately. Firstly, we construct a functor 
	$ Coh_fr^(G^or)(frak(g)^or)->Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
	Recall by Tannakian formalism, the functor of points of $frak(g)^or$ can be identified with 
	$ frak(g)^or times.o A=End_times.o (Rep G^or -->^(-times.o A)Mod_A) $
	where $End_times.o$ denotes the set of tensor endomorphisms or tensor derivations of the fiber functor. Hence, if $A$ is equipped with a $G^or$-action, a $G^or$-equivariant map of algebras $cal(O)(frak(g)^or)->A$ is equivalent to a $G^or$-equivariant map of $k$-vector spaces $(frak(g)^or)^*->A$, hence an element in $(frak(g)^or times.o A)^(G^or)$. This is simply a $G^or$-equivariant tensor endomorphism of the functor $Rep G^or->Mod_A$. 

	In our setting, we have a functor 
	$ Rep G^or->Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
	equipped with a $G$-equivariant tensor derivation given by $M$ as in @Thm_central1. Hence, by the formalism introduced in @Const_full_vers_deeq, we may first restrict to the subcategory of $attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f)$ of the form $Mod_(B,fr)^G^or$, hence by the above argument lifting to a functor $Coh_fr^(G^or)(frak(g)^or)->attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f)$. 

	We then treat the more difficult case. Now, let $A$ be a ring equipped with an action of $G^or times T^or$, that is, $A$ has a grading by $X_*(T)$ and a $G^or$-action preserving the grading. Then, a $(G^or times T^or)$-equivariant morphism $cal(O)(overline(G^or\/U^or))->A$ is a $G^or$-equivariant map 
	$ plus.o.big_(lambda in X_*(T)^+)V_lambda->A $
	compatible with the grading of $A$. The data of $V_lambda->A$ compatible with grading is equivalent to a $T^or$-equivariant map $b_lambda:V_lambda times.o A->A(lambda)$, where $A(lambda)$ is $A$ with the action of $T^or$ twisted by $lambda$, explicitly 
	$ A(lambda)_mu=A_(lambda+mu) $
	Moreover, for the above map to be a ring homomorphism, these maps must satisfy the Plücker relations. Namely, we should have 
	$ b_lambda times.o b_mu=b_(lambda+mu)(m times.o id_A):(V_lambda times.o A) times.o_A (V_mu times.o A)->A(lambda+mu) $
	Thus, again using @Const_full_vers_deeq, a functor 
	$ Coh_fr^(G^or times T^or)(overline(G^or\/U^or))->Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
	amounts to mapping the arrows 
	$ B_lambda:V_lambda times.o cal(O)->cal(O)(lambda) $
	to arrows satisfying the Plücker relations. Here, $V_lambda times.o cal(O)$ corresponds to the trivial vector bundle with fiber $V_lambda$, equipped with the diagonal $G$-action. $cal(O)(lambda)$ is the vector bundle corresponding to 
	$ plus.o.big_(mu in X_*(T)^+)V_(lambda+mu) $
	Since we know $V_lambda times.o cal(O)mapsto Z_lambda$, and $cal(O)(lambda)mapsto J_lambda=j_(t_lambda*)$, it suffices to find maps $b_lambda:Z_lambda->j_(t_lambda*)$ satisfying the Plücker relations. This will be done in the following proposition @Prop_blambda_Plucker_rel. 

	In total, we obtain the functor 
	$ Phi_1:Coh_fr^(G^or times T^or)(overline(G^or\/U^or) times frak(g)^or)->Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
]

#lemma(label-name: "Lem_supp_stalk_Z")[
	For all $lambda in X_*(T)^+$, we have the cell $Fl_t_lambda$ open in $Supp Z_lambda$, and we have an isomorphism $j_(t_lambda)^*(Z_lambda)=overline(QQ)_ell [ell(t_lambda)]$. 
]
#proof[
	Recall $Z_lambda=Z(L_lambda^Gr)$, and by the nearby cycles construction, we see 
	$ Supp Z_lambda supset pi^(-1)(Gr_(t_lambda)) $
	Then, since $Gr_lambda$ is also of dimension $ell(t_lambda)$, so $Fl_t_lambda subset pi^(-1)(Gr_lambda)$. Moreover, since the nearby cycles functor preserves the dimension of the support, we have 
	$ dim Supp Z_lambda = dim Gr_lambda = ell(t_lambda) = dim Fl_t_lambda $
	and since $Fl_t_lambda$ has dimension equal to the support, it is open. Then, since 
	$ pi:Fl_t_lambda inj Gr_lambda $
	is injective and open dense, by @Thm_central1(4) we can identify the stalk $j_(t_lambda)^*(Z_lambda)$ as the stalk of $L_lambda^Gr$ on $Gr_lambda$. This is clearly $overline(QQ)_ell [ell(t_lambda)]$. 
]

#prop(label-name: "Prop_blambda_Plucker_rel")[
	By the previous lemma, we obtain a map up to scalar 
	$ b_lambda:Z_lambda->j_(t_lambda*) $ 
	for all $lambda in X_*(T)^+$. These maps satisfy the Plücker relation. Namely, we have the commutative diagram (up to scalar)
	// https://q.uiver.app/#r=typst&q=WzAsNCxbMSwwLCJaX2xhbWJkYSpfSSBaX211Il0sWzAsMCwiWl8obGFtYmRhK211KSJdLFswLDEsImpfKGxhbWJkYSttdSkiXSxbMSwxLCJqXyhsYW1iZGEsKikqX0kgal8obXUsKikiXSxbMSwwLCJaKG1fKGxhbWJkYSxtdSkpIl0sWzIsMywidGlsZGUuZXEiXSxbMSwyLCJiXyhsYW1iZGErbXUpIiwyXSxbMCwzLCJiX2xhbWJkYSAqX0kgYl9tdSJdXQ==
	#align(center, diagram({
		node((0, 0), [$Z_lambda*_I Z_mu$])
		node((-1, 0), [$Z_(lambda+mu)$])
		node((-1, 1), [$j_(t_(lambda+mu)*)$])
		node((0, 1), [$j_(t_lambda *)*_I j_(t_mu*)$])
		edge((-1, 0), (0, 0), [$Z(m_(lambda,mu))$], label-side: left, "->")
		edge((-1, 1), (0, 1), [$tilde.eq$], label-side: left, "->")
		edge((-1, 0), (-1, 1), [$b_(lambda+mu)$], label-side: right, "->")
		edge((0, 0), (0, 1), [$b_lambda *_I b_mu$], label-side: left, "->")
	}))
]
#proof[
	Since $pi:Fl_lambda->Gr_lambda$ is an open immersion, $pi_*$ induces a fully faithful embedding on the category of sheaves. Hence, we have 
	$ Hom_(Perv_I (Fl))(Z_lambda,j_(t_lambda*))=Hom_(Perv_(L^+G)(Gr))(L_(t_lambda)^Gr,j_(t_lambda*)^Gr)=overline(QQ)_ell $
	so such $b_(lambda+mu)$ is canonical up to scalar. Thus, the above diagram commute up to scalar, and we can fix one such that the diagram really commute. 
]

We then proceed to the next step. 

#const(label-name: "Const_main_equiv_fct_step2")[
	We then upgrade $Phi_1$ to a functor 
	$ Phi_2:Coh_fr^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)->Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
	This step is relatively simple. The previous functor 
	$ Phi_1:Coh_fr^(G^or times T^or)(overline(G^or\/U^or) times frak(g)^or)->Perv^waki_I (Fl) $
	comes from an equivariant map of rings 
	$ cal(O)(overline(G^or\/U^or)times frak(g)^or)->B $
	where $B$ is obtained as in the general formalism introduced in @Const_full_vers_deeq. So, it suffices to prove the functions defining the closed subscheme $hat(cal(N))^or_aff$ is killed by the above map. This corresponds to the condition $b_lambda compose M_(Z_lambda)=0$, since the closed immersion $hat(cal(N))^or_aff subset overline(G^or\/U^or)times frak(g)^or$ can be characterized similarly. Note that $b_lambda$ is adjoint to the identity map of $overline(QQ)_ell [ell(t_lambda)]$ on $Fl_lambda$. Then, $b_lambda compose M_Z_lambda:Z_lambda->Z_lambda->j_(t_lambda*)$ is adjoint to $j_(t_lambda)^*(M_Z_lambda)$, so it suffices to prove $j_(t_lambda)^*(M_Z_lambda)=0$. This is because this map is nilpotent, but 
	$ End(j_(t_lambda)^*(Z_lambda))=End(overline(QQ)_ell [ell(t_lambda)])=overline(QQ)_ell $
	is 1-dimensional, so this map must be 0. 
	
	Thus, we have finished the construction of $Phi_2$. 
]

We now begin to do the final reduction. 

#lem(label-name: "Lem_waki_fil_gr_sym_mon")[
	The functor $gr_waki$ is monoidal. 
]
#proof[
	The monoidality follows from the general formalism of taking associated graded objects of filtered objects in Abelian categories. 
]

#prop(label-name: "Prop_waki_fil_gr_eq_res")[
	The composition 
	$ Rep G^or-->^Z Perv_I^waki (Fl)-->^(gr_waki) Rep T^or $
	is equivalent to the restriction map along the embedding $T^or inj G^or$
]
#proof[
	This will follow from the Tannakian formalism. We first show that $gr_waki compose Z$ is a symmetric monoidal functor. Recall $Z$ is central, and $gr_waki$ admits a right inverse given by the direct sum of Wakimoto sheaves. Then, let $sigma_(X,Y):Z(X)*_I Y->Y*_I Z(X)$ denote the centrality isomorphism. Since $gr_waki$ admits a right inverse, $gr_waki sigma_(X,Y)$ is a centrality isomorphism for $gr_waki compose Z$ because the naturality diagrams can be checked in $Perv_I^waki (Fl)$. 

	Now, we have $gr_waki compose Z$ is a central monoidal functor, and $Z$ is in addition symmetric, so it can be checked that the centrality isomorphism of $gr_waki compose Z$ agrees with the symmetric monoidal structure on $Rep T^or$ by the Plücker relations. Thus, we have $gr_waki compose Z$ is symmetric monoidal, and hence the functor is the restriction functor of some morphism of algebraic groups $T^or->G^or$. Again, by the highest weight line $b_lambda$ for $lambda in X_*(T)^+$, we see that $gr_waki compose Z(V_lambda)$ contains exactly one copy of $k_lambda$, hence $T^or->G^or$ is injective. 
]

This allows us to prove the following proposition. 

#prop(label-name: "Prop_waki_fil_gr_coh")[
	By construction, the image of $Phi_2$ lands in the subcategory of Wakimoto sheaves. The composition 
	$ Coh_fr^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)-->^(Phi_2)Perv_I^waki (Fl)-->^(gr_waki) Rep T^or $
	agrees with the pullback of coherent sheaves along the point $iota:(1,0)inj hat(cal(N))_aff^or$. Here $(1,0)$ denotes the $T^or$-fixed point in $hat(cal(N))_aff^or=G^or times^(U^or)frak(n)^or$ given by the base point of the vector bundle over $1 in G^or\/U^or$.  
]
#proof[
	Firstly recall that precomposing this functor with 
	$ Rep(G^or times T^or)->Coh_fr^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff) $
	gives the restriction along the diagonal map $T^or inj G^or times T^or$. Hence, we may identify the functor in question with the functor obtained via @Const_deequi_module through a $(G^or times T^or)$-equivariant morphism of rings 
	$ cal(O)(hat(cal(N))_aff^or)->overline(QQ)_ell $
	so it suffices to identify this morphism with the point $(1,0)$. Then, to determine the coordinates of this point in $frak(g)$ and $overline(G^or\/U^or)$, respectively, we must inspect where $gr_waki$ sends the morphisms $M_Z_lambda:Z_lambda->Z_lambda$ and $b_lambda:Z_lambda->j_(t_lambda*)$ to. 
	
	We first claim that the first nilpotent endomorphism is sent to 0. This is because by @Prop_Waki_cohom, we can identify the cohomology of a Wakimoto-filtered sheaf $cal(F)$ with the cohomology of $gr_waki (cal(F))$ by the parity vanishing of the cohomology of Wakimoto sheaves. So, to prove that $M_Z_lambda$ is sent to 0, it suffices to prove it is 0 on cohomology, since it can only interpolate between the same graded piece, as can be seen from the action on cohomology. Then, this map is 0 since $H^*(Fl,Z_lambda)$ can be computed by its proper pushforward to $Gr$, and the logarithm of the monodromy map on $H^*(Gr,pi_*Z_lambda)$ can be easily shown to be zero, since this monodromy can be obtained by a nearby cycles functor for the Beilinson-Drinfeld Grassmannian over a curve, but the nearby cycles functor itself is the identity map, which gives the identity monodromy, whose logarithm is 0. 

	For the second highest weight line, its associated graded is the projection onto the highest weight factor. 

	Hence, the point corresponding to the above equivariant morphism is exactly $(1,0)$. 
]

#const(label-name: "Const_main_equiv_fct_step3")[
	Now we may finally construct the final functor 
	$ Phi_3:D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or)->D(Perv_I (Fl)) $
	As in @Const_main_equiv_fct, we first take homotopy categories, and we obtain a functor 
	$ K(Phi_2):K(Coh_fr^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff))=D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)->K(Perv_I (Fl))->>D(Perv_I (Fl)) $
	Since we have (this is a version of Beuville-Laszlo's theorem) 
	$ D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)\/D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)_(partial hat(cal(N))^or)=D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or) $
	it suffices to prove the above morphism $K(Phi_2)$ vanishes on $D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)_(partial hat(cal(N))^or)$, so by the universal property of Serre quotients we obtain the final functor. 

	Since taking associated graded pieces is conservative, it suffices to prove that for any $cal(F) in D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))^or_aff)_(partial hat(cal(N))^or)$, we have $gr K(Phi_2)(cal(F)) in D Rep(T^or)$ is zero. This is because taking $gr K(Phi_2)$ amounts to taking the derived pullback 
	$ iota^*:D Coh^(G^or times T^or)(hat(cal(N))_aff^or)->D Rep(T^or) $
	which certainly vanishes on sheaves supported on $partial hat(cal(N))^or$. 
]

Thus we have finished the construction of the functor $Phi_3$, which we will now denote by $Phi$. 

== Tilting sheaves 

Now, recall from the previous section that we must prove @Thm_3_Bez_Ark and @Thm_2_Bez_Ark instead. 

#def[
	We define the functor 
	$ Phi_IW = Av_Psi compose Phi $
	to be the composition 
	$ D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or)->D(Perv_I (Fl))-> D_IW (Fl) $
	we remark that in particular this functor factors through the derived category $D(attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f))$, since the averaging functor $Av_Psi:Perv_I (Fl)->Perv_IW (Fl)$ factors through the fully faithful functor $attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f)inj Perv_IW (Fl)$. This factoring will be used to the calculation of certain Hom spaces. 

	We also define the functor 
	$ Z_IW = Av_Psi compose Z $
	to be the composition 
	$ Rep G^or->Perv_I (Fl)->Perv_IW (Fl) $
]

#def[
	We fix an interval finite poset $Lambda$, where $Lambda$ is interval finite if for any $nu<=mu in Lambda$, we have ${lambda in Lambda | nu<=lambda<=mu}$ is a finite set. In particular, the quasi-coxeter group $W^ext$ with the Bruhat order is interval finite. We equip $Lambda$ with the order topology (closed sets are the sets closed under taking smaller objects). 

	We define a highest weight category to be an $k$-linear Abelian category $cal(A)$ with the following properties: 
	+	$cal(A)$ is a finite length category. 
	+	The set of simple objects $IC(lambda)$ is indexed by $Lambda$. Moreover, we require there are some morphisms $Delta(lambda)->IC(lambda)$ and $IC(lambda)->nabla(lambda)$. They are called standard and costandard objects respectively. 
	+	$End(IC(lambda))=k$. 
	+	We let $I subset Lambda$ be closed. We define $cal(A)_I$ to be the subcategory spanned by the simple objects $IC(i)$ for $i in I$. We let $i_0 in I$ be a maximal element. Then we have the structure morphism $Delta(i_0)->IC(i_0)$ is a projective cover and $IC(i_0)->nabla(i_0)$ is an injective hull. 
	+	$ker(Delta(lambda)->IC(lambda))$ and $upright("coker")(IC(lambda)->Delta(lambda))$ lies in $cal(A)_(<lambda)$. 
	+	$Ext^2 (Delta(lambda),nabla(lambda))=0$. 

	We define a tilting object $a$ in a highest weight category to be an object equipped with both a filtration by standard objects and a filtration by costandard objects. We define the number $(a:Delta(lambda))$ to be the multiplicity of $Delta(lambda)$ in the standard filtration, and $(a:nabla(lambda))$ likewise. These numbers are actually well-defined. 
]

#rem[
	By the formalisms introduced in the paper@HighestWtShfCat, we can show that $Perv_IW (Fl)$ is a highest weight category, with standard, simple, and costandard objects given by the obvious sheaves. This is a relatively simple case, and many properties of highest weight categories follow from the functors $i_(w!)$, $i_(w*)$, $i_w^!$, and $i_w^*$ associated to the stratification. In particular, the computation $RHom(Delta_lambda,nabla_mu)=overline(QQ)_ell$ for $lambda=mu$ and 0 otherwise follows from these adjunctions. This proves the well-definedness of $(cal(F):Delta_lambda)$ and $(cal(F):nabla_lambda)$ in this case, since they are computed by $Hom(cal(F),nabla_lambda)$ and $Hom(Delta_lambda,cal(F))$. 
]

We then present the most important proposition for the proof of the equivalence @Thm_3_Bez_Ark. 

#prop(label-name: "Prop_IW_central_shv_tilting")[
	For any $V in Rep G^or$, the perverse sheaf $Z_IW (V)$ is tilting, and for any $lambda in X^or$, we have 
	$ (Z_IW (V):Delta_lambda)=(Z_IW (V):nabla_lambda)=dim(V^(T^or=lambda)) $
]

We will first prove the above proposition assuming $Z_IW (V)$ is tilting, then prove the assertion. 

#lemma(label-name: "Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_1")[
	The multiplicities in @Prop_IW_central_shv_tilting can be computed if we assume $Z_IW (V)$ is tilting. 
]
#proof[
	We prove the following equalities for $V in Rep G^or$: 
	$ sum_(i>=0)(-1)^i dim Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_mu,Z_IW (V)[i])=dim(V^(T^or=mu)) $
	$ sum_(i>=0)(-1)^i dim Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Z_IW (V)[i],nabla_mu)=dim(V^(T^or=mu)) $
	Notice that the Euler characteristic as indicated above can be computed in terms of the $K$-group, so by @Prop_compute_mult_Waki_K, we have 
	$ [Z(V)]=sum_(lambda in X_*(T))dim(V^(T^or=lambda))[J_lambda] $
	Then, since $[j_(s*)]=[j_(s!)]$ in the $K$-group, writing $lambda=mu-rho$ for $mu,rho in X_*(T)^+$, we have 
	$ [J_lambda]=[j_(t_mu*)*_I j_(t_(-rho)!)]=[j_(t_mu*)][j_(t_(-rho)*)]=[j_(t_(mu-rho)*)]=[j_(t_lambda*)] $
	by @Prop_K0_Iwahori_Fl. 
	
	To sum up, we have 
	$ [Z(V)]=sum_(lambda in X_*(T))dim(V^(T^or=lambda))[j_(t_lambda*)] $
	and hence since $Av_Psi$ is exact, we have 
	$ [Z_IW (V)]=sum_(lambda in X_*(T))dim(V^(T^or=lambda))[nabla_lambda] $
	by @Prop_basic_avr. The claim follows by highest weight category formalism, since by the Hom orthogonality and Ext vanishing, we have 
	$ sum_(i>=0)(-1)^i dim(Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_mu,nabla_nu))=delta_(mu,nu) $
	Thus we have the first equality. The second is similar. Note the computation of the Euler characteristic do not need the assumption that $Z_IW (V)$ is tilting. 

	Then, we use these equalities to compute the multiplicity $(Z_IW (V):Delta_lambda)$, and the other is similar. Now, by the tilting assumption, we have 
	$ Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_lambda,Z_IW (V)[i])=0 $
	by Ext vanishing and taking the costandard filtration for $Z_IW (V)$. On the other hand, by highest weight formalism, since $Hom(Delta_lambda,nabla_lambda)=overline(QQ)_ell$, we have
	$ dim Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_lambda,Z_IW (V))=(Z_IW (V):nabla_lambda) $
	so as a corollary we have 
	$ (Z_IW (V):nabla_lambda)=dim(V^(T^or=lambda)) $
	the other equality follows dually. 
]

Now, it suffices to prove that $Z_IW (V)$ is tilting. This is difficult, and we will achieve this through the following lemmas. 

#lem(label-name: "Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_2")[
	If $V,V' in Rep G^or$ is such that $Z_IW (V)$ and $Z_IW (V')$ are tilting, then $Z_IW (V times.o V')$ is tilting. 
]

#lem(label-name: "Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_3")[
	Assume that $G$ is semisimple. If $V$ is a highest weight $G^or$-module whose highest weight is either minuscule or quasi-minuscule, then $Z_IW (V)$ is tilting. 
]

#proof[
	We first apply the lemmas above to prove @Prop_IW_central_shv_tilting. Notice that obviously we may assume $V$ is simple. Then, for $G=G'times T'$, where $T'$ is a central torus and $G'$ semisimple, $Gr_G$ is a union of $Gr_(G')$ indexed by $X_*(T')$. The same follows for $Fl_G$ and $Fl_(G')$. Thus, the sheaf theories will not vary much, and hence we may assume firstly that $G$ is semisimple. 

	Then, the proposition would follow from that every representation of $G^or$ is a subrepresentation of minuscule or quasi-minuscule representations. This is equivalent to 
	$ plus.o.big_(lambda #[min.] \ #[or quasi-min.]) V_lambda = U $
	being a faithful representation of $G^or$. This is faithful because minuscule weights are in bijection with the center of $G^or$, so they detect the center of $G^or$ and we may assume $G^or$ is of adjoint type. Then, it suffices to prove that $U$ is a faithful $frak(g)^or$-representation. Then, we may assume $frak(g)^or$ is in fact simple by tensoring the representations for different components. We consider the quasi-minuscule representation $U'$ which has highest weight the highest short root. This gives a non-trivial representation of $frak(g)^or$, and since $frak(g)^or$ is simple, this representation is naturally faithful. 

	Thus the claim follows. 
]

#proof[
	We first prove the easier lemma in the above, that is, @Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_2. By highest weight formalism (say, in the paper@Tilting), it suffices to prove that the $!$- and $*$-pullback of $Z_IW (V times.o V')$ to each strata $i_w:Fl^w inj Fl$ is perverse (recall $Fl^w$ is the $I_0^-$-orbit). A priori, since we have $Z_IW (V times.o V')$ perverse, its $*$-pullback has perverse degree $<=0$ and its $!$-pullback lies in perverse degree $>=0$. 

	Recall $Z$ is symmetric monoidal. Then, invoking the standard filtration on $Z_IW (V)$, we have a filtration on the following 
	$ Z_IW (V times.o V')=Delta_e *_I Z(V)*_I Z(V')=Z_IW (V)*_I Z(V') $
	with subquotients $Delta_w *_I Z(V')$. Then by centrality of $Z(V')$, we have 
	$ Delta_w *_I Z(V')=Delta_0 *_I j_(w!)*_I Z(V')=Z_IW (V')*_I j_(w!) $
	Then, using the standard filtration on $Z_IW (V')$, we obtain a filtration on the above graded pieces $Z_IW (V')*_I j_(w!)$, itself with graded pieces $Delta_u*_I j_(w!)=Delta_e*_I (j_(u!)*_I j_(w!))$. 

	However, we claim that $j_(u!)*_I j_(v!)$ lies in the subcategory generated by $j_(w!)[n]$ under extensions, where $n<=0$. This is done by applying induction on the length of $v$, namely, for $ell(v)=1$ so $v$ is a simple reflection, consider the exact sequence (which is also a triangle)
	$ 0->L_e->j_(w!)->L_w->0 $
	For the case where $ell(u v)=ell(u)+1$, by @Lem_conv_I_equi_shv we are done, and if $ell(u v)=ell(u)-1$, we consider the two triangles 
	$ j_(u!)->j_(u!)*_I j_(v!)->j_(u!)*_I L_v, #h(3em) j_(u!)[-1]->j_(u!)*_I L_v->j_(u v!) $
	which successfully generates $j_(u!)*_I j_(v!)$. Thus, by applying the functor $Av_Psi$, we have a double filtration of $Z_IW (V times.o V')$ with associated graded objects $Z_IW (j_(u!)*j_(w!))$, which itself admits a filtration with associated graded objects $Delta_v [n]$ with $[n]<0$
	
	Therefore, by the adjunction $i_(w!)adj i_w^!$, we obtain the $!$-restriction of each graded pieces of $Z_IW (V times.o V')$ has perverse degree $<=0$. Dually, we have the $*$-restriction of the graded pieces of $Z_IW (V times.o V')$ has perverse degree $>=0$. Hence we are done. 
]

The more difficult lemma is @Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_3, in particular, the proof of the quasi-minuscule part is very lengthy. 

#lemma(label-name: "Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_4")[
	The statement of @Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_3 holds for minuscule weights. 
]
#proof[
	Recall that we need to prove that $Z_IW (V_lambda)$ is tilting for $lambda$ a minuscule weight. 

	In the irreducible highest weight representation $V_lambda$ with $lambda$ a minuscule weight, the finite Weyl group action on the set of weights of $V_lambda$ is transitive. Then, following @Lem_supp_stalk_Z, we have $Fl^lambda$ open in the support of $Z_IW (V_lambda)$, and the stalk on the strata is given by $Psi_lambda^*(AA SS)[ell(t_lambda)]$. Hence, we have 
	$ Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_lambda,Z_IW (V_lambda)[n])=0 $
	for $n>0$. Dually, we have 
	$ Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Z_IW (V_lambda)[n],nabla_lambda)=0 $
	for $n>0$. Now, to prove that $Z_IW (V_lambda)$ is tilting, we must prove that 
	$ Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_mu,Z_IW (V_lambda)[n])=0,Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Z_IW (V_lambda)[n],nabla_mu)=0 $
	for any $n>0$ and all $mu$. Since the only possibly non-zero parts are the $mu$'s that are weights of $V_lambda$, hence $mu in W_f lambda$, it suffices to prove that 
	$ Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_lambda,Z_IW (V_lambda)[n])=Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_(w lambda),Z_IW (V_lambda)[n]) $
	and its dual version. Then the result will follow from the first calculation. 

	For this assertion, we let $v$ be the minimal length representative in $W_f t_(w lambda)$, where $w in W_f$. Then, recall that $w t_lambda w^(-1)=t_(w lambda)$. We actually have $v=t_lambda u$, where $u in W_f$ is of minimal length such that $u w lambda=lambda$. This element satisfies $ell(v)=ell(t_lambda)-ell(u)$. Hence, we have 
	$ Delta_lambda=Delta_e *_I j_(t_lambda !)=Delta_e *_I j_(v!)*_I j_(u^(-1)!)=Delta_(w lambda)*_I j_(u^(-1) !) $
	Thus by invertibility of $j_(u^(-1) !)$, we may compute 
	$ Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_(w lambda),Z_IW (V_lambda)[n])=Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_(w lambda)*_I j_(u^(-1) !),Z_IW (V_lambda)*_I j_(u^(-1) !)[n]) $
	Then, using @Prop_basic_avr, we have
	$ Z_IW (V_lambda)*_I j_(u^(-1) !)=Delta_e *_I Z(V_lambda)*_I j_(u^(-1) !)=(Delta_e *_I j_(u^(-1) !))*_I Z(V_lambda)=Z_IW (V_lambda) $
	Thus the claim follows. 

	Hence, we have $Z_IW (V_lambda)$ tilting for $lambda$ a minuscule weight. 
]

For the quasi-minuscule case, we need some geometric prerequisites. 

#def[
	We let $cal(P)_I^0$ denote the quotient category 
	$ cal(P)_I^0=Perv_I (Fl)\/Span{L_w|ell(w)>0} $
	We denote $Pi^0$ by the projection $Pi^0:Perv_I (Fl)->cal(P)_I^0$. 

	We define $Z^0:Rep G^or->cal(P)_I^0$ to be the composition 
	$ Rep G^or->^Z Perv_I (Fl)->>cal(P)_I^0 $
	and we let $M_V^0$ denote $Pi^0(M_V)$, where $M_V$ is the nilpotent endomorphism (recall its definition in @Thm_central2). 

	Finally, we define $tilde(cal(P))_I^0$ to be the full Abelian subcategory spanned by the subquotients of the image of $Z^0$. Hence, we have $Z^0$ factors through
	$ tilde(Z)^0:Rep G^or->tilde(cal(P))_I^0 $
]

#prop(label-name: "Prop_mon_reg_quot")[
	The bifunctor 
	$ Pi^0(attach(H^0,tl:p)(-*_I-)):Perv_I (Fl)times Perv_I (Fl)->cal(P)_I^0 $
	descends to a functor 
	$ -star-:cal(P)_I^0 times cal(P)_I^0->cal(P)_I^0 $
	exact on both sides, and admits natural associativity and unitality constraints. In other words, this gives a monoidal structure on $cal(P)_I^0$. 
]
#proof[
	For the exactness of the functor, it suffices to prove the following: 
	+	For $cal(F) in Span{L_w|ell(w)>0}$ and any $cal(G) in Perv_I (Fl)$, we have $attach(H^i,tl:p)(cal(F)*_I cal(G))$ and $attach(H^i,tl:p)(cal(G)*_I cal(F))$ lying in the subcategory $Span{L_w|ell(w)}>0$. 
	+	$Pi^0(attach(H^n,tl:p)(cal(F)*_I cal(G)))=0$ for $n!=0$. 
	Then, we obtain the results for $-star-$ directly. 

	Then, for the first claim, we only prove the claim for $cal(F)*_I cal(G)$, and the other is similar. By the long exact sequence of cohomology induced by exact triangles, we may assume $cal(F)$ is of the form $L_w$ where $ell(w)>0$. We choose a simple reflection $s$ such that $ell(s w)=ell(w)-1$. Then, as in the proof of @Lem_num_id_I_equi, we pass to the category $Perv_I_s (Fl)$, where $I_s$ stands for the parahoric subgroup of $L G$. Thus, $cal(F)*_I cal(G)in D_(I_s)(Fl)$, and again as in @Lem_num_id_I_equi, any simple object that is $I_s$-equivariant cannot contain $L_u$ for $ell(u)=0$ since its orbit cannot be $I_s$-equivariant. Thus, the first claim is proven. 

	For the second claim, again assume $cal(F),cal(G)=L_w_i$ for some $w_i$ by the long exact sequence. Then, if some $w_i$ has $ell(w_i)>0$, we are done by the first claim, and otherwise if $ell(w_i)=0$, we have $L_w_i=j_(w_i !)$, and so 
	$ L_w_1 *_I L_w_2=j_(w_1 !) *_I j_(w_2 !)=j_(w_1 w_2 !)=L_(w_1 w_2) $
]

#lem[
	$Z^0:Rep G^or->cal(P)_I^0$ is a central functor. 
]
#proof[
	Recall that $Z$ and $Pi^0(attach(H^0,tl:p)(-))$ are monoidal functors, hence so is $Z^0$. Then, the centrality isomorphism for $Z$ gives 
	$ Z(V)*_I cal(F)->^tilde.eq cal(F)*_I Z(V) $
	where both sides are Perverse sheaves by @Thm_central1. Hence, applying $Pi^0$ yields the centrality isomorphism for $Z^0$. 
]

#coro[
	$tilde(cal(P))_I^0$ inherits a monoidal structure from $tilde(cal(P))_I^0 subset cal(P)_I^0$. 
]
#proof[
	This is because $Z^0$ is monoidal. Thus, let $cal(F)_i$ be a subquotient of $Z^0(V_i)$, by bi-exactness of $-star-$, $cal(F)_1 star cal(F)_2$ is a subquotient of $Z^0(V_1)star Z^0(V_2)$. 
]

We will need the Tannakian information of the functor $tilde(Z)^0$. 

#const(label-name: "Const_reg_orbit_Tan")[
	We construct a exact faithful monoidal functor 
	$ u:tilde(cal(P))_I^0->Vect_(overline(QQ)_ell) $
	By a variant of Tannakian formalism, this functor provides an equivalence 
	$ tilde(cal(P))_I^0->^tilde.eq coMod_C $
	for some bialgebra $C$ over $overline(QQ)_ell$. In addition, we will also show that the composition 
	$ Rep G^or->^tilde(Z)^0 tilde(cal(P))_I^0->^u Vect_(overline(QQ)_ell) $
	is isomorphic to the forgetful functor. 

	Again, we begin with $cal(O)(G^or) in Ind Rep G^or$. Then, consider $tilde(Z)^0(cal(O)(G^or)) in Ind tilde(cal(P))_I^0$. Recall $Ind tilde(cal(P))_I^0$ is an Abelian category. Moreover, since $cal(O)(G^or)$ is a ring object and $tilde(Z)^0$ is monoidal, we have $tilde(Z)^0(cal(O)(G^or)) in Alg(Ind tilde(cal(P))_I^0)$. Hence, we may consider its maximal left ideal object, which exists by Zorn's lemma (for any chain of left ideal objects, we may take the filtered colimit to obtain a maximal element in the chain). 

	Thus, we fix a left ideal object $cal(I) subset tilde(Z)^0(cal(O)(G^or))$. Then, writing $cal(I)$ as a subquotient of $tilde(Z)^0(V)$ ($V$ may be an ind-representation), by the commutativity constraint 
	$ tilde(Z)^0(cal(O)(G^or))star tilde(Z)^0(V)=tilde(Z)^0(V)star tilde(Z)^0(cal(O)(G^or)) $
	we have $cal(I)$ also a right ideal of $tilde(Z)^0(cal(O)(G^or))$. Hence, the quotient $tilde(Z)^0(cal(O)(G^or))\/cal(I)$ is a ring in $Ind tilde(cal(P))_I^0$, which we denote by $cal(O)(H)$. 

	By construction, $cal(O)(H)$ is simple, so $End_(Mod_(cal(O)(H)))(cal(O)(H))$ is a division algebra. In particular, by the construction of ind-categories, it is at most of countable dimension. Thus by analysing the behavior of $1\/(x-lambda)$ for $lambda in overline(QQ)_ell$, we see that this division algebra is exactly $overline(QQ)_ell$ (this process is standard). 

	Now we construct the functor. We let 
	$ u(cal(F))=Hom_(Mod_(cal(O)(H)))(cal(O)(H),cal(O)(H)star cal(F)):tilde(cal(P))_I^0->Vect_(overline(QQ)_ell) $
	First, notice that $cal(O)(H)star cal(F)$ is actually a finite free $cal(O)(H)$-module. We first prove this for $cal(F)=tilde(Z)^0(V)$ for $V in Rep G^or$. Note that there is an isomorphism 
	$ cal(O)(G^or)times.o V->cal(O)(G^or)times.o V^triv $
	where $V^triv$ is the trivial $G$-representation with the same underlying vector space as $V$. Then, applying $tilde(Z)^0$ to both sides, and then quotienting out $cal(I)$, we obtain 
	$ cal(O)(H)star tilde(Z)^0(V)tilde.eq cal(O)(H)times.o_(overline(QQ)_ell)V^triv $
	and since $cal(O)(H)$ is simple, all subquotients of $cal(O)(H)^(plus.o n)$ is simple, hence the claim. Moreover, this immediately implies $u tilde(Z)^0$ is the forgetful functor. 

	Then, by the above, we obviously have $u$ exact, and
	$ cal(O)(H)star cal(F)=cal(O)(H)times.o_(overline(QQ)_ell)u(cal(F)) $
	Hence 
	$ cal(O)(H)times.o_(overline(QQ)_ell)u(cal(F)star cal(G))=cal(O)(H)star cal(F)star cal(G)=cal(O)(H)times.o_(overline(QQ)_ell) u(cal(F))times.o u(cal(G)) $
	so $u$ is monoidal. 

	Finally, for $u$ to be faithful, it suffices to show that $u$ kills no simple object. But this is because that the simple objects are $star$-invertible, so $u$ takes them to $times.o$-invertible objects, hence not 0. 
]

#prop(label-name: "Prop_Tan_reg_quot")[
	We have 
	$ tilde(cal(P))_I^0tilde.eq Rep H $
	for some $H subset G^or$. Moreover, $H$ is contained in $Z_(G^or)(m)$ (the centralizer for the adjoint action $G^or lact frak(g)^or$) for some $m in cal(N)^or subset frak(g)^or$. 
]
#proof[
	The previous construction gives a map of bialgebras over $overline(QQ)_ell$
	$ cal(O)(G^or)->C $
	induced by $tilde(Z)^0:Rep G^or->tilde(cal(P))_I^0$. Moreover, since every object right hand side is a subquotient of the image of $tilde(Z)^0$, the map of bialgebras $cal(O)(G^or)->C$ is surjective, making $C$ a commutative bialgebra. Hence, $C$ represents a closed submonoid of $G^or$. Thus, it represents a closed subgroup $H subset G^or$ by algebraic geometry. 

	Then, for the second statement, note firstly that $M_V$ is a nilpotent tensor derivation, hence represents a nilpotent element $m in frak(g)^or$. Then, $H subset Z(m)$ is equivalent to the condition that $M_V$ gives a tensor derivation for the forgetful functor (this is because $tilde(cal(P))_I^0$ is a subquotient of $Perv_I (Fl)$)
	$ Rep G^or->Rep H tilde.eq tilde(cal(P))_I^0 $
]

#lem(label-name: "Lem_regular_nilp_elt")[
	The element $m$ is regular. 
]
#proof[
	We must compute the dimension of $V^(m=0)$ for any $V in Rep G^or$, and then take in $V=frak(g)^or$. Recall that the forgetful functor $Rep G^or->Vect_(overline(QQ)_ell)$ is given by the composition 
	$ Rep G^or->^(tilde(Z)^0)tilde(cal(P))_I^0->^u Vect_(overline(QQ)_ell) $
	Then, recall that the dimension of the kernel of $m$ is equal to the number of its Jordan blocks, so equal to $dim gr_0V+dim gr_1 V$, where $gr$ is the associated filtration of $m$ on $V$. Note that $dim u(L_w)=1$ since $L_w$ is invertible in $tilde(cal(P))_I^0$, for $u$ is monoidal. Thus, we have 
	$ dim gr_i V=sum_(omega in Omega subset X_*(T))[gr_i^w Z(V):L_omega] $
	where the associated filtration of the nilpotent operator $M_V$ on $Z(V)$ is the weight filtration, and we regard each graded piece a usual sheaf. 

	To compute the above summation, we invoke @Prop_num_Waki_wt_fil, and thus 
	$ [Z(V_lambda)]=sum_(mu in X_*(T)^+)dim V_lambda^(T^or=mu) dot theta_mu $
	hence 
	$ [Z(V)]=sum_(mu in X_*(T)^+)dim V^(T^or=mu)dot theta_mu $
	Then, consider the map $eta:cal(H)->ZZ[v,v^(-1)]$ sending $H_w mapsto (-v)^(ell(w))$. Thus, we have $eta(B_omega)=1$ for all $omega in Omega$ (or $ell(omega)=0$), and $eta(B_w)=0$ for all $ell(w)!=0$. The first identification is obvious, and for the second follows from $B_s=H_s+v$ in the case of $s$ a simple reflection, so by induction on the length, we apply @Prop_mon_reg_quot, and see that it holds for all $B_w$ for $ell(w)>0$. Moreover, we have $eta(theta_lambda)=v^(chevron.l lambda,2rho chevron.r)$ by letting $lambda=mu-nu$ for $mu,nu in X_*(T)^+$. 

	Hence, applying $eta$ we have 
	$ sum_(i in ZZ)sum_(omega in Omega)[gr_i^w (Z(V)):L_w]v^i=sum_(mu in X_*(T))dim V^(T^or=mu) dot v^(chevron.l mu,2rho chevron.r) $
	comparing both sides, we have 
	$ dim V^(m=0)=sum_(i=0,1)sum_(omega in Omega)[gr_i^w (Z(V)):L_w]=sum_(mu in X_*(T)\ chevron.l mu,2rho chevron.r in {0,1})dim V^(T^or=mu) $
	Finally, taking in $V=frak(g)^or$, we have 
	$ dim frak(Z)_(frak(g)^or)(m)=rank T^or $
	and hence $m$ is regular. 
]

#lem(label-name: "Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_5")[
	We have inequalities 
	$ dim(Hom_(Perv_IW (Fl))(Delta_e,Z_IW (V)))<=dim V^(m=0) $
	$ dim(Hom_(Perv_IW (Fl))(Z_IW (V),nabla_e))<=dim V^(m=0) $
]
#proof[
	We only prove the first inequality, since the second is analogous. 

	Recall that we have a fully faithful embedding $attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f)inj Perv_IW (Fl)$. Hence, we have 
	$ Hom_(Perv_IW (Fl))(Delta_e,Z_IW (V))=Hom_(attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f))(attach(delta_e,tl:f),attach(Z,tl:f)(V)) $
	Then, further applying $Pi^0$ gives a map 
	$ Hom_(attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f))(attach(delta_e,tl:f),attach(Z,tl:f)(V))->Hom_(cal(P)_I^0)(Z^0(overline(QQ)_ell),Z^0(V)) $
	This is injective because $attach(delta_e,tl:f)$ is a simple object not killed by $Pi^0$. 

	Finally, since we have $M_(overline(QQ)_ell)^0=0$, using that $M_V^0$ is a natural transformation we have 
	$ Hom_(cal(P)_I^0)(Z^0(overline(QQ)_ell),Z^0(V))=Hom_(cal(P)_I^0)(Z^0(overline(QQ)_ell),ker(M_V^0)) $
	Finally, applying the faithful functor $u$ we have 
	$ Hom_(cal(P)_I^0)(Z^0(overline(QQ)_ell),ker(M_V^0))inj V^(m=0) $
	hence the lemma is proven. 
]

We can finally prove the quasi-minuscule case. 

#lem[
	The statement of @Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_3 holds for quasi-minuscule weights. 
]
#proof[
	Now, we must prove $Z_IW (V_lambda)$ is tilting for $lambda$ a quasi-minuscule dominant coweight. 

	We have $Z_IW (V_lambda)$ supported on 
	$ overline(Fl^lambda)=union.big_(mu in W_f dot lambda union 0)Fl^mu $
	By the same method as in the proof of @Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_4, we obtain 
	$ Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_mu,Z_IW (V_lambda)[n])=0,Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Z_IW (V_lambda)[n],nabla_mu)=0 $
	for all $n>0$, $mu in W_f dot lambda$ It now suffices to prove the case where $mu=0$. Consider the closed embedding $i:overline(Fl^0)->Fl$, and let $j$ be its complement open. Then, consider the triangles 
	$ j_!j^*Z_IW (V)->Z_IW (V)->i_*i^*Z_IW (V) $
	$ i_*i^!Z_IW (V)->Z_IW (V)->j_*j^*Z_IW (V) $
	Since we have the tiltingness of $Z_IW (V)$ outside $overline(Fl^0)$, we have $j_!j^*Z_IW (V)$ and $j_*j^*Z_IW (V)$ perverse, hence $i^*Z_IW$ is concentrated in perverse degree 0 and -1, while $i^!Z_IW$ is concentrated in perverse degree 0 and 1. Then, by the proof of @Lem_regular_nilp_elt, we have 
	$ dim V^(m=0)=sum_(mu in X_*(T)\ chevron.l mu,2rho chevron.r in {0,1})dim V^(T^or=mu) $
	Since in our case we are treating with quasi-minuscule $V=V_lambda$, all non-zero weights appearing in $V_lambda$ are $W_f dot lambda$, and since a quasi-minuscule dominant coweight must be a coroot, we have all non-zero weights appearing in $V_lambda$ are actually coroots of $G$ (hence roots of $G^or$), so the only possible $mu$ with $chevron.l mu,2rho chevron.r in {0,1}$ is 0. 

	Hence, by @Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_5, we have 
	$ dim Hom_(Perv_IW (Fl))(Delta_e,Z_IW (V))<=dim V^(T^or=0) $
	Then by the proof of @Lem_IW_cent_tilt_lem_1, we have the identity 
	$ sum_(i>=0)(-1)^i dim Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_e,Z_IW (V)[i])=dim V^(T^or=0) $
	But then, using that $i^!Z_IW (V)$ is concentrated in perverse degrees 0 and 1, we obtain 
	$ 
	&dim Hom_(Perv_IW (Fl))(Delta_e,Z_IW (V))\ 
	<=&dim Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_e,Z_IW (V))-dim Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_e,Z_IW (V)[1]) 
	$
	so the latter term must be 0. Hence, $i^! Z_IW (V)$ is perverse. Dually, we have $i^* Z_IW (V)$ perverse, and thus $Z_IW (V)$ is tilting. 
]

== The proof of the final equivalence 

Now we may finish up the proof of @Thm_2_Bez_Ark. 

The strategy of the proof is to show full faithfulness on certain generators and then essential surjectivity. 

#prop(label-name: "Prop_final_equiv_3")[
	The category $D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or)$ is generated as a triangulated category by the following classes of objects: 
	+	The line bundles $cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)$ for $lambda in X_*(T)$. 
	+	The vector bundles $V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)$ for $V in Rep G^or$ or $lambda in X_*(T)^+$. 
	Here, recall $cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)$ can be described as the pullback of the bundle $cal(O)_(cal(B)^or)(lambda)$ from the projection $tilde(cal(N))^or->>cal(B)^or$, and $cal(O)_(cal(B)^or)(lambda)$ is the line bundle obtained from Borel-Weil-Bott theorem. 
]
#proof[
	+	For these generators, recall #h(1fr)
		$ Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or)=Coh^(G^or)(G^or times^(B^or) frak(n)^or)=Coh^(B^or)(frak(n)^or) $
		with the identification induced by the restriction along the inclusion 
		$ {1}times frak(n)^or inj tilde(cal(N))^or $
		Thus, the modules $cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)$ corresponds to the $B^or$-equivariant $cal(O)(frak(n)^or)$-modules $k_(B^or)(lambda)times.o cal(O)(frak(n)^or)$. Note that they generate the category $V times.o cal(O)(frak(n)^or)$ for $V in Rep B^or$, so it suffices to prove the latter generate the triangulated category. 

		Consider the cocharacter $2rho:GG_m->T^or$. Using restriction along this map, we regard any $T^or$- so $B^or$-equivariant module as a graded module. Then, consider the projective resolution of an $B^or$-equivariant module $M$ 
		$ dots.c->P^(-1)->P^0->M->0 $
		where each $P^j$ is of the form $V times.o cal(O)(frak(n)^or)$ for some $B^or$-representation $V$. Thus, this gives rise to a sequence of graded $cal(O)(frak(n)^or)$-modules. Since $frak(n)^or$ is a vector space, let $m$ denote its dimension. By Hilbert's syzygy theorem on dimensions of polynomial rings, $ker(P^m->P^(m-1))$ is finite free as a graded $cal(O)(frak(n)^or)$-module (apply the syzygy theorem to each grading). 

		Now, it suffices to show that any $B^or$-equivariant $cal(O)(frak(n)^or)$-module free as a $cal(O)(frak(n)^or)$-module admits a $B^or$-filtration with subquotients of the form $V times.o cal(O)(frak(n)^or)$ for $V in Rep B^or$. This is obvious by induction on the dimension. 
	+	We use these generators to reach the generators in (1). For $lambda in X_*(T)^+$, consider the highest weight line $B_lambda:V_lambda times.o cal(O)->cal(O)(lambda)$ as in @Const_main_equiv_fct_step1. This gives a surjective morphism of modules $V_lambda times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)->cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)$. Taking the Koszul complex of this surjection, we obtain an exact sequence (we set $d=dim V_lambda$)
	$ 0->and^d V_lambda->and^(d-1)V_lambda times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)->dots.c->and^1 V_lambda times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)((d-1)lambda)->cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(d lambda)->0 $
	Then, for any $mu in X_*(T)$, we pick $lambda$ such that $mu+lambda$ is dominant. Hence, tensoring the above sequence with $cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(mu)times.o(and^d V_lambda)^*$ gives the generation of $cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N)))(mu)$. 
]

We first make more use of the regularity of $m$. 

#const[
	Recall that the regular nilpotent orbit $cal(O)_r^or subset cal(N)^or$ has its preimage $tilde(cal(O))_r^or subset tilde(cal(N))^or$, and the Springer resolution restricts to an isomorphism $tilde(cal(O))_r^or->cal(O)_r^or$. Also, by the definition of orbits, $G^or\/Z_(G^or)(m)->cal(O)^or$ is an isomorphism. Thus, we have an equivalence of categories 
	$ Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(O))_r^or)tilde.eq Rep(Z_(G^or)(m)) $
	Then, let $Coh_fr^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or)$ denote the full subcategory of $Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or)$ consisting of objects of the form $V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)$, we have a functor 
	$ Coh_fr^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N)))->Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(O))_r^or)->Rep(Z_(G^or)(m))->Rep H $
	where the first functor is restriction, the second is an equivalence, and the third is induced by the restriction along the inclusion shown in @Prop_Tan_reg_quot. Moreover, the composition of the first two functors can be identified to the restriction at $tilde(m)->m$, where $tilde(m)$ is the unique preimage of $m$. 
	
	On the other hand, we have a detour given by 
	$ Coh_fr^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or)->^Phi Perv_I (Fl)->^(Pi^0)tilde(cal(P))_I^0->^(tilde.eq)Rep H $
	These two functors admit a natural monoidal structure. We claim that these two functors are actually naturally isomorphic as monoidal functors. 

	Recall we have shown in @Prop_waki_fil_gr_eq_res and @Const_reg_orbit_Tan that the two functors coincide as monoidal functors after postcomposing with the forgetful functor $Rep H->Vect_(overline(QQ)_ell)$. Thus, it suffices to show that the maps 
	$ Hom_(Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(V_1 times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or), V_2 times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or))->Hom_(Rep H)(V_1,V_2) $
	induced by the functors coincide. 

	By definition, the left hand side can be identified with $(V_1^* times.o V_2 times.o cal(O)(tilde(cal(N))^or))^(G^or)$. Then, recall that $cal(O)(tilde(cal(N))^or)=cal(O)(cal(N)^or)$, so we have a surjection $cal(O)(frak(g)^or)->>cal(O)(tilde(N)^or)=cal(O)(tilde(N)^or)$. Thus, the two functors coincide iff their precomposition with the pullback
	$ Coh_fr^(G^or)(frak(g)^or)->Coh_fr^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or)arrows Rep H $
	coincide. However, their further precomposition to 
	$ Rep G^or->Coh_fr^(G^or)(frak(g)^or)->Coh_fr^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or)arrows Rep H $
	coincide, and so an extension of this functor to functors originating from $Coh_fr^(G^or)(frak(g)^or)$ is equivalent to an tensor derivation of the map $Rep G^or->Rep H$. However, it is by construction that both tensor derivation is given by $M$. 
]

#lem(label-name: "Lem_final_equiv_1")[
	For any $V in Rep G^or$, the morphism 
	$ Hom_(D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or))->Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Phi_IW (cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)),Phi_IW (V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or))) $
	is injective. 
]
#proof[
	By definition, we have $Phi_IW (cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or))=Delta_e$, and $Phi_IW (V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or))=Z_IW (V)$. Then, using $attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f)inj Perv_IW (Fl)$ is fully faithful, it suffices to show 
	$ Hom_(Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or))->Hom_(attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f))(attach(delta_e,tl:f),attach(Z(V),tl:f)) $
	We may further apply $Pi^0$ and show 
	$ Hom_(Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or))->Hom_(tilde(cal(P))_I^0)(delta_e^0,tilde(Z)^0(V))=Hom_(Rep H)(overline(QQ)_ell,V) $
	is injective. 

	By the previous construction, we can identify this functor with the restriction at $tilde(m)$. But note that the left hand side is identified with $(V times.o cal(O)(tilde(cal(N))^or))^(G^or)$. Since $tilde(cal(O))_r^or subset tilde(cal(N))^or$ has complement of codim 2, we have $cal(O)(cal(N)^or)=cal(O)(cal(O)_r^or)$. Then, since $cal(O)_r^or=G^or\/Z_(G^or)(m)$, we have 
	$ (V times.o cal(O)(cal(O)_r^or))^G^or=(V times.o ind_(Z_(G^or)(m))^G^or)^G^or=V^(Z_(G^or)(m)) $
	which clearly embeds into $V^H$ since $H subset Z_(G^or)(m)$. 
]

#prop(label-name: "Prop_final_equiv_1")[
	For any $V in Rep G^or$, $lambda in X_*(T)^+$, and $n in ZZ$, the morphism 
	$ Hom_(D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)[n])->Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Phi_IW (cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)),Phi_IW (V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda))[n]) $ 
	is injective. 
]
#proof[
	Firstly we must treat the case where $n>0$. By the definition of equivariant derived categories, we may identify the Hom group on the left as follows. 
	$ 
	&Hom_(D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)[n]) \
	=&Hom_(D Coh^()(tilde(cal(N))^or))^G^or (cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)[n]) \
	=&(V times.o Hom_(D Coh^()(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)[n]))^G^or \
	=&(V times.o H^n (tilde(cal(N))^or,cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)))^G^or
	$
	But by the paper@Vanishcohlb, such cohomologies vanish unless $n=0$. Thus we may assume $n=0$, since the cases in the rest is trivially true. 

	Then, we find some $V$ such that $cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)inj V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)$. For $alpha in R^or_+$, consider the exact sequence of vector bundles 
	$ G^or times^(B^or)frak(n)^or_((alpha))->G^or times^(B^or)frak(n)^or->G^or times^(B^or)frak(g)_alpha^or $
	where $frak(g)_alpha^or$ is the root space, $frak(n)_(alpha)^or$ is the vector subspace of $frak(n)^or$ spanned by positive roots except $alpha$. The above exact sequence can be explicitly written out as 
	$ tilde(cal(N))_((alpha))^or->tilde(cal(N))^or->cal(O)_(cal(B)^or)(-alpha) $
	This allows us to express the ideal sheaf associated to $tilde(cal(N))_((alpha))^or$ as $p^*cal(O)_(cal(B)^or)(alpha)=cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(alpha)$. Anyway, we obtain an inclusion $cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(alpha)inj cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)$. Thus by tensoring, this holds for general $alpha in ZZ R_+^or$. Then for general $lambda$, we choose some sufficiently large $alpha in ZZ R_+^or$ such that $lambda-alpha in -X_*(T)^+$. This gives an embedding $cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)inj cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda-mu)$. Then, since $lambda-mu$ is antidominant, we have an embedding $cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda-mu)inj V_(w_0(lambda-mu))times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)$. Thus, the composition of the embeddings gives the result. 

	Finally, we have 
	$ Hom_(Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda))inj Hom_(Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),V times.o V'times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)) $
	for some $V'$. By @Lem_final_equiv_1, we have the embedding 
	$ Hom_(Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),V times.o V'times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or))inj Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Phi_IW (cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)),Phi_IW (V times.o V'times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or))) $
	Hence, we have a diagram 
	// https://q.uiver.app/#r=typst&
	#scale(89%)[
	#align(center, diagram({
		node((0, 0), [$Hom_(Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda))$])
		node((1, 0), [$Hom_(Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),V times.o V'times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or))$])
		node((1, 1), [$Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Phi_IW (cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)),Phi_IW (V times.o V'times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)))$])
		node((0, 1), [$Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Phi_IW (cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)),Phi_IW (V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)))$])
		edge((0, 0), (1, 0), "hook->")
		edge((1, 0), (1, 1), "hook->")
		edge((0, 0), (0, 1), "->")
		edge((0, 1), (1, 1), "->")
	}))]
	thus the left map must be injective. 
]

The above proposition essentially proves full faithfulness on the generators. We then move to essential surjectivity. 

#prop(label-name: "Prop_final_equiv_2")[
	The objects $Av_Psi (J_lambda)$ for $lambda in X_*(T)$ generates $D_IW (Fl)$ as a triangulated category. 
]
#proof[
	Recall $[j_(w!)]=[j_(w*)]$, so $[J_lambda]=[j_(t_lambda!)]in K^0(D_I (Fl))$. Thus, by @Prop_basic_avr, we have $[Av_Psi (J_lambda)]=[Delta_lambda]in K^0(D_IW (Fl))$. 
	
	To show the generation, it suffices to show that $Av_Psi (J_lambda)$ is supported on $overline(Fl^lambda)$ with restriction to $Fl^lambda$ of rank 1. Thus, since $Av_Psi (J_lambda)$ is perverse, its Euler characteristic of the stalk 
	$ sum_(i in ZZ)(-1)^i H^(i+ell(w))(Fl^mu,i_mu^! Av_Psi (J_lambda)) $
	is concentrated in one degree, so equal to the dimension of the stalk. Here $w$ is the minimal length element in $W_f dot t_lambda$. 

	In particular, by the equality $[Av_Psi (J_lambda)]=[Delta_lambda]$ and Euler characteristics factors through $K$-groups, this Euler characteristic is the same as the Euler characteristic 
	$ sum_(i in ZZ)(-1)^i H^(i+ell(w))(Fl^mu,i_mu^! Delta_lambda) $
	But the support and open stalk of $Delta_lambda$ is obvious. 

	Thus, by an induction on lengths, we can show that these objects generate the simple perverse sheaves, proving the generation. 
]

#proof[
	Now we will prove @Thm_3_Bez_Ark. Namely, we shall prove that 
	$ Phi_IW: D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or)->D_IW (Fl) $
	is an equivalence of categories. 

	First, essential surjectivity follows from @Prop_final_equiv_2 and full faithfulness, since by definition the image of $Phi_IW$ contains the image of $Av_Psi compose Phi_0$, and $Phi_0 (V_0 times.o k_lambda)=J_lambda$, so $Av_Psi (J_lambda)$ is contained in the image of $Phi_IW$. 

	Then we show full faithfulness. We temporarily denote the following map by $f$, and we will show 
	$ f:Hom_(D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(F),cal(G))->Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Phi_IW (cal(F)),Phi_IW (cal(G))) $
	is an isomorphism. 

	We first show the injectivity for the case $cal(F)=cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)$ and $cal(G)=V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)[n]$, where $V in Rep G^or$ and $lambda in X_*(T)^+$. Then, this map is an injection by @Prop_final_equiv_1. We claim the source and target of $f$ are finite dimensional $overline(QQ)_ell$-vector spaces of the same dimension, which will prove that $f$ is an isomorphism. 
	
	For the right hand side, it can be identified with 
	$ 
	&Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_e,Z_IW (V)*_I J_lambda [n]) \
	=&Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_e*_I J_(-lambda),Z_IW (V)[n]) \
	=&Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_(-lambda),Z_IW (V)[n])
	$
	Thus by @Prop_IW_central_shv_tilting, this is zero unless $n=0$, and for $n=0$ the dimension is $dim V^(T^or=-lambda)$. 

	For the left hand side, in the proof of @Prop_final_equiv_1 we have shown that it vanishes unless $n=0$, and for $n=0$, it can be identified with 
	$ (V times.o H^0(tilde(cal(N))^or,cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)))^(G^or) $
	So its dimension can be computed by the simple factors, namely, it is of dimension 
	$ sum_(nu in X_*(T)^+) [V^*:V_nu]dot[H^0(tilde(cal(N))^or,cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)):V_nu] $
	Again by the paper@Vanishcohlb, the second factor is $dim V_nu^(T^or=lambda)$, so 
	$ sum_(nu in X_*(T)^+)[V^*:V_nu]dot dim V_nu^(T^or = lambda)=dim (V^*)^(T^or=lambda)=dim V^(T^or=-lambda) $
	Thus this part is proven. 

	Now, since $V times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)$ generates the whole triangulated category by @Prop_final_equiv_3, we have finished the proof that $f$ is isomorphic for $cal(F)=cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)$ and $cal(G)$ any sheaf. Now, take $cal(F)=cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)$ for all possible $lambda in X_*(T)$, we have 
	$ Hom_(D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda),cal(G))=Hom_(D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),cal(G)times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(-lambda)) $
	and 
	$ 
	&Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Phi_IW (cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)),Phi_IW (cal(G))) \
	=&Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_e *_I J_lambda,Phi_IW (cal(G))) \
	=&Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Delta_e,Phi_IW (cal(G)) *_I J_(-lambda)) \
	=&Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Phi_IW (cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)),Phi_IW (cal(G)times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(-lambda)))
	$
	by the monoidality of $Phi_IW$. Thus, since we have an isomorphism  
	$ Hom_(D Coh^(G^or)(tilde(cal(N))^or))(cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or),cal(G)times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(-lambda))=Hom_(D_IW (Fl))(Phi_IW (cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)),Phi_IW (cal(G)times.o cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(-lambda))) $
	by the case for $cal(F)=cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)$, the case where $cal(F)=cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)$ and arbitrary $cal(G)$ follows. Then, since $cal(O)_(tilde(cal(N))^or)(lambda)$ also generates the triangulated category by @Prop_final_equiv_3, we have finished the proof of full faithfulness.
	
	Thus, $Phi_IW$ is an equivalence of categories! 
]

#proof[
	We now prove @Thm_2_Bez_Ark, namely, 
	$ Av_Psi:attach(cal(P)_I,tl:f)->^tilde.eq Perv_IW (Fl) $
	Recall we have proven in @Prop_comp_I_IW_1st_half that $Av_Psi$ is fully faithful. To prove essential surjectivity, note by @Thm_3_Bez_Ark that every $cal(F) in Perv_IW (Fl)$, there is some $cal(G) in D_I (Fl)$ such that $Av_Psi (cal(G))=cal(F)$. Thus, 
	$ cal(F)=Av_Psi (attach(H^0,tl:p)(cal(G)))=Av_Psi (attach((attach(H^0,tl:p)(cal(G))),tl:f)) $
	so essential surjectivity follows. 
]
